#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
claude-mate-wrap — wrap `claude` in a PTY and report its live state.
=====================================================================

Run `claude` THROUGH this wrapper (alias claude=claude-mate-wrap). It:

  1. Forks a pseudo-terminal and runs the real `claude` inside it, relaying
     stdin/stdout transparently — you use Claude exactly as normal.
  2. Mirrors the full TUI into a headless terminal emulator (pyte) and reads the
     *current screen* every update, so it sees EVERYTHING: the spinner, API
     errors/retries, permission prompts, and every BACKGROUND-WORK tell Claude
     renders once the foreground turn ends — the "Waiting for N dynamic workflow
     to finish" banner, the turn recap's "· 1 shell still running" suffix, and
     the live in-flight chip in the hint row ("… · 1 shell · ← for agents"),
     which is the one that also covers work that is merely QUEUED (i.e. it knows
     the session is still busy after the turn "ends"). The tells Claude prints
     into the TRANSCRIPT count only while they are still its last word: printed
     text never rewrites itself, so trusting a stale copy — a recap still reading
     "1 shell still running" after that shell exited — pinned WIP for good.
  3. Reports state changes to the Claude Mate daemon over its Unix socket, and
     auto-registers on start / removes on exit (the display mirrors your open
     wrapped terminals).
  4. Listens on a per-session control socket for window commands, auto-picking
     the method from $TERM_PROGRAM (iTerm2 / Terminal.app / VS Code / tmux /
     generic): 'focus' raises (and un-minimizes) THIS terminal window — selects
     the session's tab and brings its window to the front (macOS Automation, no
     Accessibility permission). Terminal.app's activate+frontmost has a one-raise
     lag that surfaces the wrong window, so there we skip activate when Terminal
     is already frontmost and front our exact window directly (de-miniaturize if
     collapsed, else set-frontmost) — sent on the GO button;
     'collapse' miniaturizes it — sent when the card moves on. Commands are
     acked in two stages — 'go' on receipt (liveness), 'ok' only after the
     window op completes — so the daemon can order window ops ACROSS sessions
     (unordered osascripts finish out of order and leave a stale terminal on
     top) without stalling long on a wedged wrapper.

State model (matches the daemon): working | waiting | error | idle
  working  TUI is generating, OR background work (a dynamic workflow, a
           background agent, a backgrounded shell, a monitor, an MCP task) is
           still running or queued.
  waiting  a permission prompt / question is on screen (needs your input).
  error    an API error / retry is visible.
  idle     at the prompt, nothing pending.

Account profiles: each subdirectory of ~/.claude-accounts is a separate Claude
Code login (used as that session's CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR), so different terminals
can run different accounts at once. When profile dirs exist, an interactive
start shows a picker (Enter = default ~/.claude login); `--account NAME` or
$CLAUDE_MATE_ACCOUNT skips it, and an already-set $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR always
wins. `mkdir ~/.claude-accounts/work` (or picking a new name at the prompt)
creates a profile; claude asks /login there on first run. No profile dirs ->
no picker, nothing changes.

Account + remaining limit on the device: the wrapper also reports WHICH account
this session runs as (the profile name, or 'default') and how much of that
account's plan limit is LEFT -- a background thread reads the session's OAuth
token (never writes it) and polls Anthropic's usage endpoint every
CLAUDE_MATE_USAGE_POLL_S seconds. The daemon shows the account on the state row
and the remaining-limit chip (e.g. '5h82%' = 82% of the 5-hour window left,
'wk31%' = 31% of the week left -- whichever window is more depleted) on the
model+effort row.

Dynamic workflows on the device: a workflow (the Workflow tool) runs on after
the turn that launched it has ended, and its row in the panel under the prompt
box is the one live sign of it. The wrapper reads those rows -- the status from
the bullet's colour, the progress, the run's own clock, the tokens, a
usage-limit pause -- and every report carries up to three of them, or '-' when
there are none (see wf_field). It is a scrape of an undocumented footer, so it
is best effort: a row it cannot read is a row it does not report.

Carrying a conversation to another account: an account is a $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR,
read once at startup, so a session can never change account in place -- and each
profile keeps its own projects/ tree, so a new one cannot see the old one's
transcripts. `claude-mate-switch` closes that gap: this wrapper publishes a
per-terminal context file (which transcript is in flight, under which account,
in which directory) under $TMPDIR, and the switch copies that transcript into
the target profile and re-execs `claude --resume`. Temp and per-terminal on
purpose -- it describes a running process, is worthless once that process is
gone, and two terminals in one directory are two different conversations.

Config (env):
  CLAUDE_MATE_SOCK          daemon socket (default /tmp/claude-mate.sock)
  CLAUDE_MATE_DEBUG         if set to a path, append screen+state snapshots there
  CLAUDE_REAL               explicit path to the real `claude` binary (else autodetected)
  CLAUDE_MATE_ACCOUNTS_DIR  profile root (default ~/.claude-accounts)
  CLAUDE_MATE_ACCOUNT       profile name to use without showing the picker
  CLAUDE_MATE_USAGE_POLL_S  remaining-limit poll period (default 120; <=0 disables)

Requires: pyte  (pip install pyte)
"""
import os, sys, pty, tty, termios, fcntl, struct, signal, select, socket, \
       threading, time, re, json, uuid, subprocess, shutil, atexit, hashlib, \
       tempfile, colorsys, unicodedata

try:
    import pyte
except ImportError:
    # Defer the fallback. The OLD code here did `os.execvp("claude", ...)`, but in
    # the global-shim deployment `claude` IS this wrapper -> it re-exec'd itself
    # forever (the "never block the user" path doing exactly that). main() instead
    # execs real_claude() -- the same shim-skipping resolver the wrapped path uses
    # -- before it touches anything pyte-dependent.
    pyte = None

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Config / identity
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
DAEMON_SOCK = os.environ.get("CLAUDE_MATE_SOCK", "/tmp/claude-mate.sock")
DEBUG = os.environ.get("CLAUDE_MATE_DEBUG")
SID = uuid.uuid4().hex[:8]
NAME = (os.path.basename(os.getcwd()) or "claude")[:20]
TERM_PROGRAM = os.environ.get("TERM_PROGRAM", "")
CTRL_SOCK = f"/tmp/claude-mate-ctrl-{SID}.sock"
TITLE = f"◆cm:{NAME}:{SID}"          # unique terminal title (generic-focus fallback)
try:
    MY_TTY = os.ttyname(0)           # this terminal's device — used for EXACT focus
except OSError:
    MY_TTY = ""

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# State-detection patterns. Case-insensitive substrings matched against the
# rendered screen. Defaults are deliberately TIGHT (only unambiguous signals) so
# incidental words don't false-trigger. Override LIVE — no restart — by editing
# the JSON at CLAUDE_MATE_PATTERNS (default: <repo>/patterns.json) with keys
# "error" / "waiting" / "busy" -> lists of substrings.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
DEFAULT_PATTERNS = {
    # STRONG error signals -- specific enough to match in the wider status region
    # (a real error box is multi-line). "api error" is Claude's own retry banner;
    # the others are unambiguous UI strings. (Bare "rate limit" / "rate_limit_error"
    # are deliberately NOT here: they are ordinary code/doc tokens that show up on
    # screen as content and would false-trigger the whole-screen scrape.)
    # The last five are how 2.1.295 ends a turn on a limit -- its own list of
    # limit-row openings: "You've hit your 5-hour limit · resets 3pm", "You've
    # reached your Fable limit.", "You're out of usage credits", "Your org is
    # out of usage · add funds to continue". "usage limit reached" now appears
    # only on a retry line and on the grey auto-continue notice. Above the
    # prompt box every phrase counts only where it OPENS a row in Claude's error
    # colours (colour_phrases), so a reply that says them is safe.
    "error": [
        "api error", "overloaded_error",
        "service is temporarily",
        "usage limit reached", "you've hit your", "you've reached your",
        "you're out of usage credits", "you're out of extra usage",
        "your org is out of usage",
    ],
    # CONTENT-PRONE error PHRASES -- specific enough to mean a real failure but
    # still possible in ordinary output, so matched ONLY in the tight live status
    # region (bottom BUSY_TAIL_LINES) and only when NOT actively working. Bare HTTP
    # status codes (" 500" etc.) are deliberately NOT here: they collide with the
    # live token meter ("↓ 500 tokens") and with any number on screen, reading as a
    # false 'error' that then nags every 5s. A genuine API error carries "api error"
    # (the strong list above) or one of these phrases.
    "error_footer": [
        "internal server error", "connection error", "request timed out",
    ],
    # Specific prompt text -- safe to match anywhere on the screen.
    "waiting": [
        "do you want to proceed", "yes, and don't ask",
        "tell claude what to do differently", "no, and tell claude",
        "press enter to continue",
    ],
    # Interactive option/menu picker footers (AskUserQuestion, file/option
    # selectors) mean WAITING for your choice -- e.g. "Enter to select ·
    # Tab/Arrow keys to navigate · Esc to cancel". These words are GENERIC, so
    # detect_state matches them ONLY in the footer (last few non-empty lines),
    # never against conversation/file content higher up the screen, AND a footer
    # match must PERSIST (WAITING_FOOTER_DEBOUNCE_S) before it reports as waiting.
    # NOTE: "esc to cancel" / "esc to go back" are intentionally absent -- the
    # /effort and /model sliders carry that footer, so matching it buzzed the
    # instant you opened one. A real question picker carries "to select" /
    # "to navigate"; those config sliders do not.
    "waiting_footer": [
        "to select", "to navigate",
    ],
    # Signals that appear ONLY when genuinely generating: the interrupt hint
    # ("esc to interrupt", distinct from a picker's "esc to cancel") and the
    # "running in the background" footer.
    #
    # The end-of-turn banner's phrases ("background agent", "agent to finish",
    # "workflow to finish", "tool to finish") used to live here too. They are the
    # SAME signal _BG_WORK_RE matches, but as bare substrings they carry neither
    # its structural shape nor the _fresh() gate -- so once Claude had printed
    # "Waiting for 1 background agent to finish" into the transcript, any frame
    # that still showed that line read as working, agent long since returned.
    # (The idle-prompt gate does not save it: Claude's hint row alternates, and
    # in the captured session it read "(shift+tab to cycle)", not "? for
    # shortcuts".) _BG_WORK_RE covers every one of them properly.
    "busy": [
        "esc to interrupt", "running in the background",
    ],
}

_PATTERNS_FILE = os.environ.get(
    "CLAUDE_MATE_PATTERNS",
    os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))),
                 "patterns.json"),
)
_patterns = {k: [s.lower() for s in v] for k, v in DEFAULT_PATTERNS.items()}
_patterns_mtime = [0.0]

def load_patterns():
    """Hot-reload patterns from the JSON file if it changed (live tuning)."""
    try:
        m = os.stat(_PATTERNS_FILE).st_mtime
    except OSError:
        return
    if m == _patterns_mtime[0]:
        return
    try:
        with open(_PATTERNS_FILE) as fh:
            data = json.load(fh)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return
    new = {k: [s.lower() for s in v] for k, v in DEFAULT_PATTERNS.items()}
    for k in ("error", "error_footer", "waiting", "waiting_footer", "busy"):
        if isinstance(data.get(k), list):
            new[k] = [str(x).lower() for x in data[k]]
    # Atomic publish: rebind the module-global to the freshly built dict in one
    # store, rather than clear()+update() (which a concurrent reader could observe
    # half-empty). Detection now runs on a single thread (see monitor()), but the
    # atomic swap keeps this correct regardless.
    global _patterns
    _patterns = new
    _patterns_mtime[0] = m

POLL_MS = 0.25            # re-evaluate the screen this often (quiescence)
DEBOUNCE_S = 0.30         # require a state to persist before reporting it
# A "waiting" detected ONLY from the generic option-picker footer must persist
# this long before we report it. A real AskUserQuestion/permission picker sits
# there until you answer; a config dialog you open and dismiss (/effort, /model,
# /config) is gone in ~1s -- so this debounce stops those transient pickers from
# buzzing while still catching genuine prompts (just ~2.5s later, which is fine
# for an away-from-keyboard alert). Specific waiting text (permission prompts)
# is unaffected -- it still reports at DEBOUNCE_S.
WAITING_FOOTER_DEBOUNCE_S = 2.5
KEEPALIVE_S = 20.0        # re-send current state periodically

# How many non-empty bottom lines count as Claude's "live status region". State
# signals (error/prompt/generating) are matched only here, so the same keywords
# in scrollback / logs / code / conversation above don't false-trigger.
STATUS_TAIL_LINES = 20    # error/waiting region (error boxes & permission prompts
                          # are genuinely multi-line, so they need the wider window)
BUSY_TAIL_LINES = 8       # tighter region for the live spinner: the generating meter,
                          # the 'esc to interrupt' hint, and background-agent notices
                          # only ever render on the bottom status line(s). A narrow
                          # window keeps a meter STRING quoted higher up in the
                          # conversation (e.g. these very design notes) from reading
                          # as "working" while the session is actually idle.
FOOTER_LINES = 4          # tighter region for the generic picker-footer phrases

# Claude's IDLE-at-prompt footer hint. It shows only when the session is waiting
# for input; a genuinely working frame -- foreground OR a running agents panel --
# shows "esc to interrupt" instead, never this. So when it is in the bottom footer
# we know the session is idle, and a spinner-SHAPED line elsewhere on screen (a
# pasted status line, docs/blockquotes about the meter, this repo's own test
# fixtures) is quoted content, not a live spinner -- it must not read as working.
_IDLE_FOOTER = ("? for shortcuts",)

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Terminal size helpers
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def get_winsize(fd):
    try:
        data = fcntl.ioctl(fd, termios.TIOCGWINSZ, b"\0" * 8)
        rows, cols, _, _ = struct.unpack("HHHH", data)
        if rows and cols:
            return rows, cols
    except Exception:
        pass
    return 24, 80

def set_winsize(fd, rows, cols):
    try:
        fcntl.ioctl(fd, termios.TIOCSWINSZ, struct.pack("HHHH", rows, cols, 0, 0))
    except Exception:
        pass

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Daemon reporting
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def _field(value):
    """One field of a report. A '|' would split it in two -- shifting every
    field after it, the workflows into the limit's place -- and a CR or LF
    would end the line early, so neither survives. Anything not text is ''."""
    if not isinstance(value, str):
        return ""
    return value.replace("|", "/").replace("\r", "").replace("\n", "")

def send_daemon(state):
    """Best-effort one-line report to the daemon. Never raises.

    Wire format, always all nine fields:
        state|SID|NAME|CTRL_SOCK|MODEL|EFFORT|ACCOUNT|LIMIT|WF
    (model/effort are empty until captured from the screen; account/limit are
    empty until known. WF is this session's dynamic workflows: '-' for none,
    or up to three '~'-joined records
        st;done;total;failed;pct;elapsed_s;tokens;note;name
    -- see wf_field -- or '' when they could not be read this time, which the
    daemon takes as "keep what you have". The daemon treats ALL trailing fields
    as optional, so older peers stay compatible.) NAME is the directory's own
    name, so it is sanitised like the rest: a '|' in it used to shift every
    field after it."""
    head = "|".join([state, SID, _field(NAME), CTRL_SOCK]
                    + [_field(g_meta[k]) for k in ("model", "effort",
                                                   "account", "limit")])
    try:
        wf = _field(g_meta.get("wf", ""))
    except Exception:                  # never at the cost of the state report
        wf = ""
    try:
        c = socket.socket(socket.AF_UNIX, socket.SOCK_STREAM)
        c.settimeout(0.5)
        c.connect(DAEMON_SOCK)
        # errors="replace": a directory name that is not valid UTF-8 reaches
        # Python as lone surrogates, which a strict encode refuses -- and a
        # refused report is a session the device never hears from again.
        c.sendall(f"{head}|{wf}\n".encode("utf-8", errors="replace"))
        c.close()
    except OSError:
        pass

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Focus: raise THIS terminal window, method chosen from $TERM_PROGRAM
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def _osa(script, timeout=4):
    try:
        subprocess.run(["osascript", "-e", script],
                       stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=timeout)
    except Exception:
        pass

def focus_self():
    # If we're inside tmux, also select our pane/window.
    pane = os.environ.get("TMUX_PANE")
    if os.environ.get("TMUX") and pane:
        for args in (["tmux", "select-window", "-t", pane],
                     ["tmux", "select-pane", "-t", pane]):
            try:
                subprocess.run(args, stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                               stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=3)
            except Exception:
                pass

    tp = TERM_PROGRAM.lower()
    if tp == "iterm.app":
        # Match the session by its TTY (exact), fall back to title. Restore the
        # window first in case the nav preview miniaturized it.
        _osa(f'''
            tell application "iTerm2"
              activate
              repeat with w in windows
                repeat with t in tabs of w
                  repeat with s in sessions of t
                    try
                      if (tty of s) is "{MY_TTY}" or (name of s contains "{TITLE}") then
                        set miniaturized of w to false
                        select w
                        tell t to select
                        select s
                        return
                      end if
                    end try
                  end repeat
                end repeat
              end repeat
            end tell''')
    elif tp == "apple_terminal":
        # Raise THIS window (matched by its TTY) to the front of Terminal.app,
        # reliably, with NO Accessibility permission.
        #
        # The naive `activate` + `set frontmost of w to true` is broken: after
        # `activate`, Terminal fronts whatever its *current key window* is and a
        # `set frontmost` only lands on the NEXT `activate` -- a one-raise LAG
        # that surfaces the window you picked LAST time (raise #3 brings #2
        # forward). `activate` is the whole problem: it re-keys Terminal's own
        # last window and stomps our selection. So we AVOID it whenever we can
        # and never let it fight our selection:
        #   - Only `activate` when Terminal is NOT already the frontmost app
        #     (needed just to cross over from another app on GO). Reading which
        #     process is frontmost via System Events needs no Accessibility --
        #     only *manipulating* windows through it would.
        #   - Then front OUR window directly, with no `activate` after it to
        #     re-key: de-miniaturize it if it's collapsed (the one op that
        #     dependably fronts a minimized window), else `set frontmost` (which
        #     is reliable for a visible window once `activate` isn't stomping it).
        # No miniaturize/restore dance -> no window flicker. Note "front window"
        # here means the lowest-index NON-miniaturized window; a miniaturized
        # window can still report index 1, so don't gate on index.
        _osa(f'''
            tell application "System Events"
              set termFront to ((name of first process whose frontmost is true) is "Terminal")
            end tell
            if not termFront then
              tell application "Terminal" to activate
              repeat 30 times
                delay 0.03
                tell application "System Events"
                  if ((name of first process whose frontmost is true) is "Terminal") then exit repeat
                end tell
              end repeat
            end if
            tell application "Terminal"
              set tg to missing value
              repeat with w in windows
                repeat with t in tabs of w
                  try
                    if (tty of t) is "{MY_TTY}" then
                      set selected of t to true
                      set tg to w
                      exit repeat
                    end if
                  end try
                end repeat
                if tg is not missing value then exit repeat
              end repeat
              if tg is missing value then return
              if miniaturized of tg then
                set miniaturized of tg to false
              else
                set frontmost of tg to true
              end if
            end tell''', timeout=8)
    elif tp == "vscode":
        # Can't target a specific integrated-terminal pane externally; raise VS Code.
        _osa('tell application "Visual Studio Code" to activate')
    elif tp == "ghostty":
        _osa('tell application "Ghostty" to activate')
    elif tp in ("warpterminal", "warp"):
        _osa('tell application "Warp" to activate')
    else:
        # Generic: ask System Events to raise any window whose title matches
        # (un-minimizing it first in case the nav preview collapsed it).
        _osa(f'''
            tell application "System Events"
              set procs to (every process whose background only is false)
              repeat with p in procs
                repeat with w in (windows of p)
                  if name of w contains "{TITLE}" then
                    try
                      set value of attribute "AXMinimized" of w to false
                    end try
                    set frontmost of p to true
                    perform action "AXRaise" of w
                  end if
                end repeat
              end repeat
            end tell''')

def collapse_self():
    """Miniaturize THIS session's terminal window (the daemon's SCROLL-nav
    preview collapses the previously-shown tab as the card moves on).
    Best effort, mirrors focus_self()'s per-$TERM_PROGRAM matching. No-ops
    where a single pane can't be collapsed (tmux panes, VS Code integrated
    terminals) -- collapsing the whole shared window would hide innocent
    bystanders."""
    if os.environ.get("TMUX"):
        return                              # a tmux pane can't be miniaturized
    tp = TERM_PROGRAM.lower()
    if tp == "iterm.app":
        _osa(f'''
            tell application "iTerm2"
              repeat with w in windows
                repeat with t in tabs of w
                  repeat with s in sessions of t
                    try
                      if (tty of s) is "{MY_TTY}" or (name of s contains "{TITLE}") then
                        set miniaturized of w to true
                        return
                      end if
                    end try
                  end repeat
                end repeat
              end repeat
            end tell''')
    elif tp == "apple_terminal":
        _osa(f'''
            tell application "Terminal"
              repeat with w in windows
                repeat with t in tabs of w
                  try
                    if (tty of t) is "{MY_TTY}" then
                      set miniaturized of w to true
                      return
                    end if
                  end try
                end repeat
              end repeat
            end tell''')
    elif tp == "vscode":
        pass                                # can't collapse one integrated pane
    else:
        # Generic: minimize any window whose title matches ours.
        _osa(f'''
            tell application "System Events"
              set procs to (every process whose background only is false)
              repeat with p in procs
                repeat with w in (windows of p)
                  if name of w contains "{TITLE}" then
                    try
                      set value of attribute "AXMinimized" of w to true
                    end try
                  end if
                end repeat
              end repeat
            end tell''')

def ctrl_listener():
    """Per-session socket: the daemon connects and sends 'focus' (raise +
    un-minimize this terminal) or 'collapse' (miniaturize it). Two-stage
    reply: 'go' immediately on receipt (proves this wrapper is alive, so the
    daemon's short first wait doesn't burn its full op deadline on a wedged
    process), then 'ok' only AFTER the window op completes. The daemon holds
    one window-op lock across ALL sessions and waits for 'ok', so ops apply
    in exactly the order the device drove them -- fire-and-forget osascripts
    from different wrappers finish out of order, which left a stale terminal
    expanded (device card != laptop window)."""
    try:
        if os.path.exists(CTRL_SOCK):
            os.unlink(CTRL_SOCK)
        srv = socket.socket(socket.AF_UNIX, socket.SOCK_STREAM)
        # 0600, NOT 0666. This socket could always be asked for the rendered
        # screen; it can now also be asked to TYPE INTO the session, which turns
        # "any local account can read your terminal" into "any local account can
        # act as you inside Claude". The daemon runs as the same user (it is a
        # per-user LaunchAgent), so nothing legitimate needs group or other.
        #
        # The umask is what makes it 0600 *from the moment it exists*. chmod
        # alone left a window -- bind() creates the node with the process umask,
        # normally 022, which for a socket is srwxr-xr-x: world-CONNECTABLE
        # until the next line ran. Microseconds, on a path an attacker would
        # have to be watching for, and still the wrong way round: permissions
        # that are corrected are permissions that were briefly wrong. The chmod
        # stays as the belt to that pair of braces (a umask of 0 in some exotic
        # parent, an fs that ignores it).
        old_umask = os.umask(0o177)
        try:
            srv.bind(CTRL_SOCK)
        finally:
            os.umask(old_umask)
        os.chmod(CTRL_SOCK, 0o600)
        srv.listen(4)
    except OSError:
        return
    while True:
        try:
            conn, _ = srv.accept()
        except OSError:
            break
        try:
            try:
                # 512, not 64: `submit <text>` carries a payload, and a silently
                # truncated command would type half a line into the session.
                data = conn.recv(512).decode(errors="ignore").strip()
            except OSError:
                data = ""
            if data.startswith("screen"):
                # Mirror request: hand back the rendered TUI so the device can
                # show what the session is actually doing. Length-prefixed
                # rather than terminated by a sentinel, because terminal output
                # can contain any line -- including one that is exactly "ok".
                rows = g_screen_text.split("\n") if g_screen_text else []
                try:
                    conn.sendall(("\n".join(["SCREEN %d" % len(rows)] + rows
                                            + ["ok"]) + "\n")
                                 .encode("utf-8", "replace"))
                except OSError:
                    pass
            elif data.startswith("submit "):
                # Type a line into the session and press Enter -- the device's
                # way to say "continue" after a limit or an error, without
                # reaching for the laptop. The text is whatever follows; the CR
                # is added here so the verb means one whole thing and a caller
                # can never half-submit.
                #
                # Deliberately NOT a general remote-keyboard: control bytes are
                # dropped, so this cannot send Ctrl-C, an escape sequence, or a
                # bracketed-paste marker into the TUI.
                text = "".join(c for c in data[7:] if c.isprintable())
                if text:
                    queue_input(text.encode("utf-8", "replace") + b"\r")
                try:
                    conn.sendall(b"ok\n")
                except OSError:
                    pass
            elif data == "switch" or data.startswith("switch "):
                # Continue this conversation on another account. Exact verb, not
                # a prefix: everything else here matches loosely, but this one
                # ENDS the session, so it must never be triggered by a command
                # that merely starts with the same six letters.
                #
                # Checked before the child is touched, and refused with 'err' so
                # the daemon can say why. A switch kills claude to do its work;
                # discovering afterwards that it cannot proceed costs the session
                # for nothing -- which is what a device press into a fresh
                # session used to do (claude died, then "no transcript recorded
                # for this terminal yet", and the terminal was back at a shell).
                global g_switch_req
                req = data[6:].strip() or "--best"
                why = switch_refusal(req)
                if why:
                    try:
                        conn.sendall(f"err {why}\n".encode("utf-8", "replace"))
                    except OSError:
                        pass
                else:
                    g_switch_req = req
                    try:
                        conn.sendall(b"ok\n")
                    except OSError:
                        pass
                    if g_child_pid > 0:
                        try:
                            os.kill(g_child_pid, signal.SIGTERM)
                        except OSError:
                            pass
            else:
                known = data.startswith("focus") or data.startswith("collapse")
                if known:
                    try:
                        conn.sendall(b"go\n")   # liveness: accepted, now running
                    except OSError:             # -- the daemon's short first wait
                        pass                    # ends here, not at its deadline
                    if data.startswith("focus"):
                        focus_self()
                    else:
                        collapse_self()
                try:
                    conn.sendall(b"ok\n")   # ack: op done, the next one may run
                except OSError:
                    pass
        finally:
            try:
                conn.close()
            except OSError:
                pass

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Model + effort capture from the rendered screen
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Claude's TUI shows the model and effort in a couple of reliable spots:
#   - the welcome box header line, e.g.
#       "│   Opus 4.8 (1M context) with xhigh · Claude Max ·   │"
#   - an effort "pill" above the prompt / in the /model dialog, e.g.
#       "◉ xhigh · /effort"   /   "◉ xHigh effort ←/→ to adjust"
# Neither is guaranteed to stay on-screen once a long conversation scrolls, so
# we capture opportunistically and CACHE the last seen values per session (the
# model is captured at boot from the welcome box; effort is refreshed whenever
# the pill is visible, e.g. after you change it). Both are scoped tightly so the
# same words appearing in ordinary conversation/code can't be mistaken for them:
#   - the model must sit on the welcome-box header line specifically: inside a
#     "│" border AND followed (same bordered segment) by the "Claude <plan>"
#     badge it always carries -- e.g. "│ Opus 4.8 (1M context) with xhigh ·
#     Claude Max · │". Requiring "Claude" rules out a bordered line that merely
#     mentions a model, e.g. a /model picker row "│ 2. Opus 4.8 · capable │" or a
#     markdown-table cell, which would otherwise overwrite the sticky cache.
#   - the effort must directly follow the "◉" pill glyph and be a known level.
_MODEL_RE = re.compile(
    r'│[^│\n]*?\b((?:Opus|Sonnet|Haiku|Fable)\s+[0-9.]+)\b[^│\n]*?claude', re.I)
_EFFORT_RE = re.compile(
    r'◉\s*(low|medium|high|xhigh|max|ultracode)\b', re.I)

# Last-known model/effort for THIS session (sticky cache), plus the account
# it runs as (set once at startup), the remaining-limit chip (refreshed by
# the usage poller thread) and the workflow field (rebuilt by evaluate() on
# every pass; '-' until a workflow has been seen). All of them ride along on
# every daemon report.
g_meta = {"model": "", "effort": "", "account": "", "limit": "", "wf": "-"}

# The rendered TUI as plain text, republished by evaluate() on every pass. The
# device's mirror view is served from THIS rather than by reaching into the pyte
# screen from the ctrl thread: `screen`/`screen_lock` are locals of main(), and
# taking that lock off-thread self-deadlocks against the feed loop (see the note
# by the resize handler). Rebinding a str is atomic under the GIL, the same
# atomic-publish trick used for _patterns, so no lock is needed to read it.
g_screen_text = ""

# Text the device asked us to type into the session, waiting for the relay loop
# to hand it to the child's PTY. The ctrl thread cannot write to master_fd
# itself: the relay owns that fd, drains it non-blocking, and keeps a backlog in
# `pend` -- a second writer would interleave mid-sequence and corrupt a paste.
# So the ctrl thread queues here and pokes the relay's self-pipe to wake it.
g_inject = bytearray()
g_inject_lock = threading.Lock()
g_wake_w = -1                      # relay's self-pipe write end; set by main()

# A switch asked for from the device. The wrapper OWNS the claude process, so it
# is the only thing that can replace it -- hence the request lands here, the
# child is asked to exit, and the exec happens on the way out (see main's tail),
# after cleanup has restored the terminal. Holding the request also tells
# cleanup() to LEAVE the context file in place: the switcher is about to read it
# to find out which conversation to carry.
g_switch_req = ""
g_child_pid = -1

# What this session IS, for the tracker and for anything that has to recreate
# it: the conversation id we handed claude (see session_argv) and the flags the
# user started with. The flags matter -- a switch that resumes without them
# silently drops --dangerously-skip-permissions, --model, --effort and the rest,
# so you land in a different session than the one you were carrying.
g_claude_sid = ""
g_user_argv = []
g_started_at = 0.0

def queue_input(data: bytes):
    """Hand keystrokes to the relay from another thread."""
    with g_inject_lock:
        g_inject.extend(data)
    if g_wake_w >= 0:
        try:
            os.write(g_wake_w, b"\0")   # break select() out of its 1s wait
        except OSError:
            pass

def drain_inject(pend):
    """Move queued device input into the relay's outbound buffer.

    A function rather than three lines inline in the relay, because it is the
    only part of the path a test can reach: the rest of main() needs a real
    controlling terminal (it calls tcsetattr, which raises SIGTTOU and stops the
    process anywhere else), so an end-to-end test of typing would be testing the
    harness. This seam is the mechanism itself."""
    if not g_inject:
        return False
    with g_inject_lock:
        pend.extend(g_inject)
        del g_inject[:]
    return True

def parse_meta(screen_text):
    """Refresh g_meta from the current screen if model/effort are visible."""
    m = _MODEL_RE.search(screen_text)
    if m:
        g_meta["model"] = " ".join(m.group(1).split())   # collapse stray spaces
    e = _EFFORT_RE.search(screen_text)
    if e:
        g_meta["effort"] = e.group(1).lower()

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# State detection from the rendered screen
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Claude Code's live ACTIVITY METER is shown in EVERY actively-working frame --
# while generating, running tools, AND during the extended-thinking phase -- e.g.
#   "✻ Forming… (14s · ↓ 610 tokens · thinking with xhigh effort)"
#   "✻ Herding…  (2m 3s · ↑ 1,204 tokens · esc to interrupt)"
# The spinner verb rotates and the "esc to interrupt" hint ROTATES OUT for a
# "Tip:" line every few seconds, so keying "working" off "esc to interrupt"
# alone mis-reads the thinking phase (and the tip frames) as idle -- which the
# daemon then turns into a premature DONE buzz mid-turn. The meter itself is the
# stable signal: an arrow+token counter, or an elapsed-timer parenthetical that
# also carries "tokens", or the "thinking with <effort> effort" suffix. All three
# are specific enough that ordinary prose rarely matches -- and because the live
# meter is matched only within the bottom BUSY_TAIL_LINES status region (see
# detect_state), a meter string QUOTED higher up in the conversation (such as in
# these comments) sits outside that window and no longer false-reads as "working".
_WORKING_METER_RE = re.compile(
    r'[↑↓]\s*[\d.,]+\s*[kKmM]?\s*tokens?\b'   # ↓ 610 tokens / ↓ 79.9k tokens / ↑ 1,204 tokens
    r'|\(\s*\d+\s*[hms]\b[^)\n]*\btokens?\b'  # (14s · … tokens …) / (1h 4m 30s · … tokens)
    r'|\bthinking with\s+\w+\s+effort\b',     # thinking with xhigh effort
    re.I)

# A LIVE SPINNER line, matched over the WHOLE visible screen (not just the bottom
# region). Claude renders one while generating AND while a background agent /
# workflow runs -- a spinner VERB ending in an ellipsis, then a parenthetical with
# a running elapsed timer and either an arrow token meter or the thinking hint:
#   "✻ Forming… (14s · ↓ 610 tokens · thinking with xhigh effort)"
#   "· Hatching… (5m 2s · ↓ 15.3k tokens)"       (a subagent spawning)
#   "✶ Working… (3s · ↑ 429 tokens · esc to interrupt)"
# When a background agent's transcript fills the screen BELOW it, that spinner sits
# ABOVE the bottom BUSY_TAIL_LINES window -- so keying "working" only off the bottom
# region mis-read a busy multi-agent session as idle (-> premature DONE).
#
# It stays quote-resistant WITHOUT a region limit via TWO structural requirements
# an adversarial review found necessary (a bare "glyph + timer + meter" match false-
# fired on markdown tables `| Opus | 2m 30s | ↓ 79.9k tokens |`, bulleted meter
# quotes `- … 3m 30s … ↓ 79.9k tokens`, and finished-agent recap rows):
#   1. a spinner VERB immediately followed by an ellipsis then "(<timer> …" -- prose,
#      tables, bullets and "○ … done · …" recaps have no "word…(timer" shape;
#   2. it must sit at the LINE START (only optional indent + one spinner glyph) --
#      so a spinner string QUOTED mid-sentence ('the meter reads "✻ Forming… (14s…)"')
#      is not at a line start and does not match.
_LIVE_SPINNER_RE = re.compile(
    r'^[ \t]*[^\w\s]?[ \t]*'                   # line start: optional indent + one glyph
    r'\w+[ \t]*(?:…|\.\.\.)[ \t]*'             # a spinner verb + ellipsis: "Forming…"
    r'\(\s*\d+\s*[hms]\b[^)\n]*?'              # "(14s …" / "(1h 4m …" running elapsed timer
    r'(?:[↑↓]\s*[\d.,]+\s*[kKmM]?\s*tokens?'   # … then an arrow token meter, OR
    r'|thinking with\s+\w+\s+effort)',         # … the thinking-effort hint
    re.I | re.M)

# Claude's END-OF-TURN BACKGROUND-WORK banner. The foreground reply has finished
# (the prompt is available again) but real work is STILL running -- a dynamic
# workflow, background agents, or a tool. Claude prints a line like
#   "Waiting for 1 dynamic workflow to finish"
#   "Waiting for 2 background agents to finish"
# Two things make it different from the foreground spinner, and both matter here:
#   1. it can sit FAR above the bottom region -- the task panel + the prompt box
#      push it up, well outside BUSY_TAIL_LINES -- so it is matched over the whole
#      TRANSCRIPT (this is exactly why a genuinely-busy session was mis-read idle
#      -> premature DONE);
#   2. it COEXISTS with the idle prompt ("? for shortcuts" shows while background
#      work runs), so it must NOT be suppressed by the idle-prompt gate.
# Quote-resistance is structural instead: it must sit at a LINE START (optional
# indent + one glyph) and match the exact "Waiting for <N> ... to finish" shape,
# so prose about background work ("still waiting for the agent to finish") never
# fires -- there is no leading count, or "waiting" is not the first word.
#
# It is TRANSCRIPT text, not a live element -- see _fresh() below, which is what
# stops it claiming a finished agent forever.
#
# What the count must be followed by is one of the banner's OWN nouns, with the
# trailing "to finish" optional -- which covers both problems at once:
#   * the banner WRAPS in a narrow terminal ("Waiting for 2 background agents and
#     1 dynamic workflow to finish" is 64 columns before Claude's own padding),
#     dropping "to finish" onto the next screen line and losing the match;
#   * a free-form "waiting for <N> ... to finish" also matches Claude's ordinary
#     PROSE -- "⏺ Waiting for 2 CI jobs to finish before I merge." is a
#     line-start sentence with a count and "to finish" -- and prose is permanent
#     transcript that no later frame ever clears.
_BG_WORK_RE = re.compile(
    r'^[ \t]*[^\w\s]?[ \t]*'                   # line start: optional indent + one glyph
    r'waiting for\s+\d+\s+'                    # "Waiting for 1 " (a live count)
    r'(?:background\s+)?'                      # "... 2 background agents ..."
    r'(?:agents?|dynamic workflows?|workflows?|tools?|tasks?)\b'   # the banner's own nouns
    r'(?:[^\n]*?\bto finish\b)?',              # ...trailing clause, absent when it WRAPS
    re.I | re.M)

# Claude's TURN-DURATION line, when the turn ended with background work still
# registered but NOT counted into the banner above (a backgrounded shell, a
# monitor, an MCP task, a teammate -- anything that is not an agent/workflow the
# banner names). It reads:
#
#   "✻ Sautéed for 2m 33s · 1 background dynamic workflow still running"
#   "✻ Cooked for 45s · 2 shells still running"
#
# Same rationale as the banner: it can sit far above the bottom region and it
# coexists with the idle prompt, so it is matched over the whole transcript.
# Quote-resistance is the shape of the line itself -- a verb + "for <elapsed>" at
# a line start, then a "·" separator, then "still running" -- which prose about a
# process ("the dev server is still running") does not have. Like the banner it
# is TRANSCRIPT text and needs the _fresh() gate below.
_BG_TURN_END_RE = re.compile(
    r'^[ \t]*[^\w\s]?[ \t]*'                   # line start: optional indent + one glyph
    r'\w+[ \t]+for[ \t]+\d+\s*(?:ms|[hms])\b'  # "Sautéed for 2m 33s" (past-tense recap)
    r'[^\n]*?·[^\n]*?\bstill running\b',       # "... · 2 shells still running"
    re.I | re.M)

# Claude's LIVE IN-FLIGHT CHIP in the hint row under the prompt box. Whenever
# background work is registered -- RUNNING **or PENDING** -- Claude adds a dim,
# dot-separated chip naming it, and it is there in BOTH states (captured from a
# real session, background shell in flight):
#
#   busy: "⏵⏵ bypass permissions on · 1 shell · esc to interrupt · ← for agents"
#   idle: "⏵⏵ bypass permissions on · 1 shell · ← for agents · ↓ to manage"
#
# This is the tell that stays TRUE. The banner and the turn recap above are
# transcript text -- printed once, never updated, scrolled away by the next
# output -- whereas the chip is redrawn every frame and disappears the instant
# the last task ends. It is also what queued-but-not-yet-started work looks like
# on screen: the session the device kept reading as IDLE, buzzing DONE while the
# work ran on.
#
# The chip's wording is Claude's own summary of the task set, so the phrases are
# enumerated rather than guessed. Quote-resistance comes from WHERE it is read:
# only BELOW the prompt box (see split_screen), which is Claude's own chrome --
# neither the conversation nor the line you are typing into can reach it.
_BG_CHIP_RE = re.compile(
    r'(?:·[ \t]*|^[ \t]{0,4})\d+[ \t]+'        # a "· <N> " chip (or one opening the row)
    r'(?:shells?|monitors?|local agents?|teams?'
    r'|MCP (?:task|job)s?|background (?:tasks?|dynamic workflows?)'
    r'|remote dynamic workflows?|cloud sessions?|loops?)\b',
    re.I | re.M)

# The same fact on Claude's compact status row, which renders a dim, RIGHT-
# ALIGNED "<N> in background" instead of the chip. Cheap to also accept.
#
# Quote-resistance: the row carries NOTHING but the counter -- right alignment
# puts only padding in front of it and it ends the line -- so prose ("2 in
# background mode", "leaves 3 in background.") does not fit the shape, and
# neither does a right-aligned table column that happens to read the same
# ("  build                              2 in background"), which the earlier
# "line start OR 2+ spaces" form accepted.
_BG_COUNT_RE = re.compile(r'^[ \t]*\d+ in background[ \t]*$', re.I | re.M)

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Transcript vs live chrome — the two halves of the screen, and their freshness
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Claude's prompt box cuts the visible screen in two, and the halves have
# OPPOSITE trust properties:
#   * ABOVE it is the TRANSCRIPT — what Claude has already printed. Those lines
#     are frozen: nothing rewrites them, and while you are away from the keyboard
#     nothing scrolls them off either.
#   * BELOW it is LIVE CHROME — the hint row and, when subagents exist, the
#     agents panel. Claude redraws it every frame, so it stops asserting a fact
#     the moment the fact stops being true, and no conversation text can reach it.
# Each background-work tell is read against the half it actually lives in.
# The prompt box's TOP rule can carry a label at its right end -- captured live
# in 2.1.295: "──────…────── ultracode ─". Unmatched, the box's top was missed,
# the line you are typing landed in the transcript and in the meter region, and
# a "↓ 600.8k tokens" typed there read as live work.
_RULE_RE = re.compile(r'^[ \t]*[╭╰┌└├]?[─━═╌┄┈╍]{8,}'
                      r'(?:[ ][^─━═\n]{1,40}?[ ][─━═╌┄┈╍]{1,4})?'
                      r'[╮╯┐┘┤]?[ \t]*$')
PROMPT_BOX_MAX_LINES = 8      # widest gap split_screen walks up between the box's rules

def split_screen(nonblank):
    """(transcript, chrome) — the screen either side of Claude's prompt box.

    `transcript` is everything ABOVE the box; `chrome` is everything below the
    box's LAST rule, which deliberately excludes the input line itself so text
    you are typing ("kill the 3 shells") is never read as Claude's own chrome.
    Returns (None, None) when no prompt box is on screen — a full-screen picker
    or dialog — and callers then fall back to the plain bottom-of-screen window.
    """
    rules = [i for i, ln in enumerate(nonblank) if _RULE_RE.match(ln)]
    if not rules:
        return None, None
    bottom = top = rules[-1]
    for i in reversed(rules[:-1]):     # walk up over the box's own rule(s)
        if top - i > PROMPT_BOX_MAX_LINES:
            break
        top = i
    return nonblank[:top], nonblank[bottom + 1:]

# Whether Claude's NORMAL prompt footer is on screen -- the one place a missing
# workflow row means the panel really has no rows. split_screen() accepts any
# rule, and every dialog draws one (a permission prompt's top border, the
# workflow detail view's header), so on its own it cannot tell a dialog from
# the prompt. The prompt box is two rules with the input line between them
# ("❯ ...", "! ..." in shell mode). And with the box up, the panel under it can
# still be swapped out: help ("? "), shell mode, the "/" and "@" suggestion
# lists, exit-confirm -- each replaces the hint row, and the workflow rows go
# with it while the runs go on.
_INPUT_RE = re.compile(r'^[ \t]*[❯>!][ \t\xa0]')
_FOOTER_SWAPPED_RE = re.compile(
    r'! for (?:shell|bash) mode|/ for commands|@ for file paths|again to exit'
    r'|^[ \t]{2}[/@][\w:./-]+[ \t]{2,}\S', re.M)

def prompt_footer_up(nonblank):
    """True when the frame shows the prompt box (two rules, the input line
    between them) with Claude's ordinary footer below it.

    At any height: the box grows with what you type -- a long prompt wraps,
    a pasted list keeps its lines -- so the input line right under the top
    rule is what makes two rules the box, not how close together they are.
    Held to PROMPT_BOX_MAX_LINES, a prompt of eight lines or more read as the
    footer away: the workflow rows under it were held instead of read, and a
    run that finished there went on being reported as running, its clock
    ticking, for as long as the prompt stayed that long. A pair of rules
    with anything else under the top one is no box, however close."""
    rules = [i for i, ln in enumerate(nonblank) if _RULE_RE.match(ln)]
    for k in range(len(rules) - 1, 0, -1):
        top, bottom = rules[k - 1], rules[k]
        inner = nonblank[top + 1:bottom]
        if not inner or not _INPUT_RE.match(inner[0]):
            continue
        if inner[0].lstrip().startswith("!"):
            return False                       # shell mode: rows hidden
        return not _FOOTER_SWAPPED_RE.search("\n".join(nonblank[bottom + 1:]))
    return False

# A transcript tell is only true while it is still Claude's LAST word on the
# subject. Both end-of-turn tells above are printed INTO the transcript, so they
# never stop claiming what they claimed. Captured from live sessions (the frames
# are in tools/test_detect.py):
#
#   shell  t=13.9s  "✻ Worked for 4s · 1 shell still running"      <- printed
#          t=57.7s  `sleep 45` exits; the hint-row chip vanishes; the line stays
#          t=210s   ...the same line, verbatim, session idle at the prompt
#   agent  t=16.3s  "✻ Waiting for 1 background agent to finish"   <- printed
#          t=25.9s  "⏺ Agent ... finished · 9s" prints BELOW it; the line stays
#          t=190s   ...the same line, verbatim, session idle at the prompt
#
# Both sessions read "working" for the entire capture. The daemon only alerts
# DONE on working -> idle, so the device sat on WIP for good and never buzzed --
# and nothing scrolls those lines away while you are away, which is exactly when
# the device is the only thing you can see.
#
# The gate is structural rather than a timeout: when the work really ends Claude
# SAYS so on a new line ("⏺ Background command ... completed (exit code 0)",
# "⏺ Agent ... finished · 9s"), and any further reply is a bullet or a fresh turn
# recap too -- so a tell expires on the very frame its work does. Turn-end
# furniture that legitimately follows a tell while work is still in flight (the
# task panel, the "❋ recap:" line, a usage warning) carries none of these shapes
# and expires nothing.
_NEW_OUTPUT_RE = re.compile(
    r'^[ \t]*(?:[⏺●⎿][ \t]'                    # a reply / tool-call / tool-result bullet
    r'|[^\w\s]?[ \t]*\w+[ \t]+for[ \t]+\d+\s*(?:ms|[hms])\b)',   # a LATER turn recap
    re.M)

def _fresh(pattern, transcript_text):
    """True when `pattern` matches the transcript AND nothing Claude printed
    after it has superseded it. Uses the LAST match, so an older copy higher up
    the screen can never veto a freshly reprinted tell."""
    last = None
    for last in pattern.finditer(transcript_text):
        pass
    return (last is not None
            and not _NEW_OUTPUT_RE.search(transcript_text[last.end():]))

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Dynamic-workflow rows — the one live tell a running workflow leaves
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# A dynamic workflow (the Workflow tool) keeps running after the turn that
# launched it has ended, and from then on exactly ONE thing on screen says so:
# its row in the panel Claude draws BELOW the prompt box. Captured from the live
# session that was reported (Claude Code 2.1.295), device on DONE:
#
#   "  ◯ workflow-status-design-revi…  ▱▱▱▱▱▱▱▱▱▱▱▱▱▱▱▱▱▱▱▱  0/5 · 1m01s · ↓ 491.6k tokens"
#
# Nothing else fired. The hint-row chip that names every other kind of
# background work (_BG_CHIP_RE) leaves local workflows out -- Claude filters them
# from it -- and the "Waiting for 1 dynamic workflow to finish" banner had not
# been printed at all. So the turn's end read IDLE, and the daemon turned that
# into DONE one minute into the run.
#
# The layout, from Claude Code's footer code: a 2-column pointer field ("  ", or
# "❯ " on the row you have selected), the bullet (◯; ⏸ while the run waits out a
# usage limit), the name padded to a shared column (<= 28 columns, cut with "…"),
# two spaces, a 20/12/8-cell bar (▰▱; █░ on Ghostty; "NN%" for a screen reader),
# two spaces, then " · "-joined segments: "done/total", the run's clock ("45s",
# "2m39s", "1h02m", "1d03h"), "↓ 542.8k tokens". They drop out as the terminal
# narrows -- done/total FIRST (gone below ~85 columns for a long name), then the
# word "tokens", the clock, the count -- until only the bar is left. A paused row
# has no bar at all:
#   "  ⏸ nightly-triage  paused · usage limit resets at 3:30pm"
# A row counts only with a bar directly after the name (or, for ⏸, the word
# "paused") and nothing but known segments after that -- an agent row
# ("◯ Explore  Count .h files ...") has neither, and nor has the hint row's
# permission-mode indicator, which draws the SAME glyph: "⏸ plan mode on
# (shift+tab to cycle)". And only BELOW the prompt box (split_screen's chrome),
# where no conversation text and no line you are typing can reach.
#
# The row's STATUS is not in its text at all. It is the bullet's colour: none
# while running; success = completed; error = failed or stopped, OR still
# running with a failed agent; warning (with ⏸) = paused on a usage limit. A
# finished row lingers ~30 s, clock frozen, before Claude drops it -- read as
# text alone it would hold a finished session on WORK for that long -- so
# evaluate() reads the bullet's colour off the emulator cells
# (wf_bullet_colours) and hands it in.
_WF_ROW_RE = re.compile(
    r'^(?P<sel>[ ]{2}|[❯>][ ])'                    # pointer field: "  " or "❯ "
    r'(?:(?P<pz>⏸)|[◯○]|\( \))[ ]+'                # bullet: ⏸ paused, ◯ otherwise
    r'(?P<name>\S+(?: \S+)*?)'                     # name: never two spaces in a row
    r'(?(pz)'
      r'(?:[ ]{2,}(?P<paused>paused(?: · .*?)?))?'  # "paused · usage limit resets …"
    r'|'
      r'[ ]{2,}(?P<bar>[▰▱]{1,20}|[█░]{1,20}|\d{1,3}%)(?P<cut>…)?'
      r'(?:[ ]{2}(?P<segs>\S.*?))?'
    r')'
    r'[ ]*$')
_WF_SEG_DONE = re.compile(r'^(\d{1,6})/(\d{1,6})$')
_WF_SEG_CLOCK = re.compile(r'^(?:(\d{1,4})d(\d{2})h|(\d{1,2})h(\d{2})m|(\d{1,2})m(\d{2})s'
                           r'|(\d{1,2})s)$')
_WF_SEG_TOKENS = re.compile(r'^↓ (\d{1,9}(?:\.\d{1,3})?)([kmbt]?)(?: tokens)?$')
_WF_MULT = {"": 1, "k": 10 ** 3, "m": 10 ** 6, "b": 10 ** 9, "t": 10 ** 12}
_WF_BULLET_COL = 2        # "  ◯ name" / "❯ ◯ name": the bullet always sits here
_WF_LIVE = "REP"          # statuses that are still in flight (see wf_status)
WF_FROZEN_S = 10.0        # a red row's clock this still -> it has ended (see below)
WF_REDRAW_S = 1.5         # rows missing this briefly under the footer were being
                          # redrawn, not dropped (WorkflowWatch, 5)
# What the daemon is sent about them (wf_field): a report must stay one short
# line, and the device has 14 characters for a note and 40 for a name.
WF_MAX_RECORDS = 3        # workflows per report: the device shows one, counts the rest
WF_NAME_MAX = 40          # characters of a name
WF_NOTE_MAX = 14          # characters of a usage-limit note
WF_FIELD_MAX = 300        # UTF-8 bytes of the whole field
WF_JUST_ENDED_S = 10.0    # a run seen to end this recently goes out first: the
                          # daemon's WF_SPOTLIGHT_S, the time its band shows it
_WF_TZ_RE = re.compile(r'[ ]*\([^()]*\)?[ ]*$')   # " (Europe/Madrid)", even cut short

def _wf_note(text):
    """A paused row's segments -> its note, short enough for the device.

    The device has 14 characters for it, and Claude's wording does not fit:
        "paused · usage limit resets at 3:30pm"                 -> "til 3:30pm"
        "paused · usage limit resets at 3:30pm (Europe/Madrid)" -> "til 3:30pm"
        "paused · usage limit resets Oct 11, 3pm"               -> "til Oct 11 3pm"
        "paused · usage limit"                                  -> "usage limit"
        "paused"                                                -> ""
    When the pause ends is the part worth keeping, so the time zone goes
    first, then the "til", and only then the end of the time itself. ASCII
    only -- the device draws nothing else -- and never one of the wire's own
    separators (; ~ |)."""
    if "resets" not in text:
        return "usage limit" if "usage limit" in text else ""
    when = "".join(" " if c.isspace() else c
                   for c in text.split("resets", 1)[1]
                   if c.isspace() or (" " < c < "\x7f" and c not in ";~|"))
    when = when.strip()
    if when.startswith("at "):
        when = when[3:]
    when = " ".join(_WF_TZ_RE.sub("", when).replace(",", "").split())
    if not when:                               # "resets" and nothing after it
        return "usage limit"
    note = "til " + when
    return (note if len(note) <= WF_NOTE_MAX else when)[:WF_NOTE_MAX]

def parse_wf_row(line):
    """One footer line -> the workflow row it is, or None.

    Returns {name, paused, done, total, pct, clock, elapsed_s, tokens, note};
    any field the row does not show (the terminal was too narrow, or the run is
    paused) is None. `pct` is read off the bar when done/total has been dropped.
    `note` is a paused row's usage-limit note, already cut down for the device
    (_wf_note); '' on every other row.
    """
    m = _WF_ROW_RE.match(line)
    if m is None:
        return None
    row = {"name": m.group("name"), "paused": m.group("pz") is not None,
           "done": None, "total": None, "pct": None, "clock": None,
           "elapsed_s": None, "tokens": None, "note": "",
           # "⏸ name" with nothing after it: Claude's narrowest paused row --
           # and the exact shape of the hint row's mode indicator ("⏸ plan mode
           # on (shift+tab to cycle)"). Only its amber bullet tells them apart,
           # so detect_state takes a bare row only with that colour in hand.
           "bare": False}
    if row["paused"]:
        row["bare"] = m.group("paused") is None
        row["note"] = _wf_note(m.group("paused") or "")
        return row
    bar = m.group("bar")
    if bar.endswith("%"):
        row["pct"] = min(100, int(bar[:-1]))
    else:
        width = len(bar) + (1 if m.group("cut") else 0)
        row["pct"] = (100 * sum(1 for c in bar if c in "▰█")) // width
    segs = m.group("segs")
    for seg in (segs.split(" · ") if segs else ()):
        seg = seg.strip()
        if seg.startswith("⚠") or seg == "/workflows to stop":
            continue                           # the size warning, in its own colour
        d = _WF_SEG_DONE.match(seg)
        if d:
            row["done"], row["total"] = int(d.group(1)), int(d.group(2))
            continue
        c = _WF_SEG_CLOCK.match(seg)
        if c:
            g = [int(x) if x else 0 for x in c.groups()]
            row["clock"] = seg
            row["elapsed_s"] = (g[0] * 86400 + g[1] * 3600 + g[2] * 3600
                                + g[3] * 60 + g[4] * 60 + g[5] + g[6])
            continue
        t = _WF_SEG_TOKENS.match(seg)
        if t:
            try:
                row["tokens"] = int(round(float(t.group(1)) * _WF_MULT[t.group(2)]))
            except (ValueError, OverflowError):
                return None
            continue
        return None                            # an unknown segment: not a workflow row
    if row["done"] is not None and row["total"]:
        row["pct"] = (100 * row["done"]) // row["total"]
    return row

# Claude Code's bullet colours are theme colours, and the six themes draw them
# as truecolor, 256-colour or the 16 named ANSI colours (pyte reports those by
# name, yellow as "brown"). Hue bands separate the three roles in every one of
# them -- including the colour-blind themes, whose "success" is blue -- and
# anything grey or unset is "no colour", which is what a running row has.
_WF_NAMED = {"red": "error", "brightred": "error", "magenta": "error",
             "brightmagenta": "error", "green": "success",
             "brightgreen": "success", "blue": "success", "brightblue": "success",
             "cyan": "success", "brightcyan": "success", "brown": "warning",
             "brightbrown": "warning", "yellow": "warning",
             "brightyellow": "warning"}

def _wf_role(fg):
    """A pyte fg -> 'success' | 'error' | 'warning' | '' (no colour)."""
    if not fg or fg == "default":
        return ""
    role = _WF_NAMED.get(fg)
    if role is not None:
        return role
    try:
        r, g, b = (int(fg[i:i + 2], 16) / 255.0 for i in (0, 2, 4))
    except (ValueError, TypeError):
        return ""
    hue, sat, _val = colorsys.rgb_to_hsv(r, g, b)
    if sat < 0.25:
        return ""
    deg = hue * 360.0
    if deg < 15 or deg >= 300:
        return "error"
    if deg < 75:
        return "warning"
    return "success"

def wf_status(row, role):
    """R running | E running, an agent failed | P paused | D completed | F ended
    without completing. From one frame an error-coloured row is taken as still
    running (E); only WorkflowWatch, which sees its clock stop, can say F."""
    if row["paused"] or role == "warning":
        return "P"
    if role == "success":
        return "D"
    if role == "error":
        return "E"
    return "R"

def wf_bullet_colours(screen, display):
    """{display line: (bullet fg, name fg)} for every line that could be a
    workflow row. Call under screen_lock -- the buffer is live. Two cell reads
    per candidate row, so it costs nothing on the 0.25 s tick.

    Both cells, because a running row is not uncoloured: Claude draws its
    bullet and its name in the same dimmed colour -- the theme's "inactive",
    grey in every built-in theme but any colour a custom theme likes -- and
    only a status (success, error, warning) gives the bullet a colour of its
    own. So a bullet the colour of its own name is a running row, whatever
    the hue (_wf_bullet_role)."""
    out = {}
    buf = screen.buffer
    for y, line in enumerate(display):
        if len(line) > _WF_BULLET_COL and line[_WF_BULLET_COL] in "◯○⏸(":
            name_col = _WF_BULLET_COL + (4 if line[_WF_BULLET_COL] == "(" else 2)
            try:
                out[line] = (buf[y][_WF_BULLET_COL].fg, buf[y][name_col].fg)
            except Exception:
                pass
    return out

def _wf_bullet_role(entry):
    """wf_bullet_colours' entry for a row -> its bullet's role. A bare fg (no
    name colour to compare with) is classified on its own."""
    if isinstance(entry, tuple):
        bullet, name = entry
        if bullet == name:
            return ""                          # dimmed with its name: running
        return _wf_role(bullet)
    return _wf_role(entry)

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Error phrases: Claude's own error UI is coloured, prose that mentions it is not
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# The strong "error" phrases are matched in the bottom 20 lines, and those lines
# are mostly CONVERSATION. Reported: the device sat on ERR because the reply on
# screen explained, in so many words, that "a workflow named after 'the api
# error' read ERROR" -- any reply about an API, or a log quoted near the bottom,
# could do the same.
#
# Claude Code draws every error it reports in a colour: an API-error row
# ("⏺ API Error: 529 {...}") in the warning colour, glyph and text; the live
# retry line's error in the error colour ("✻ API error · Retrying in 5s ·
# attempt 1/10" -- from the third attempt on it shows the error itself, "529
# Overloaded", which none of the phrases names); a limit row ("⎿  You've hit
# your session limit · resets 3pm") in either. A reply, a quoted log or the line
# you are typing is in the plain text colour -- but colour alone is not enough:
# a code block's string literals are red, a diff's strings Monokai yellow, the
# spinner draws the current task in Claude's orange, failed tool output is red.
# What none of those does is START a row with the phrase: an error row opens
# with it, right after its glyph, while code, diffs, spinner text and agent rows
# open with something else. So a phrase counts only at the start of a row's
# text (after the indent and at most one glyph); above the prompt box's lower
# rule it must also start on an error- or warning-coloured cell (unless the
# screen has no colour at all), and the row must still be current: a prompt you
# have submitted since (its echo, "❯ continue") puts an old error behind you.
# Below the box is Claude's own chrome (its limit notices live there): the
# start-of-row rule alone.
_ALERT_ROLES = ("error", "warning")
_ROW_START_RE = re.compile(r'^[ \t\xa0]*(?:[^\w\s][ \t\xa0]+)?$')   # indent + one glyph
_PROMPT_ECHO_RE = re.compile(r'^[ \t]*[❯>][ \t\xa0]+\S')

def _display_columns(screen, y):
    """String index in pyte's display line y -> its cell column. A wide
    character takes two cells but one string position; mirrors Screen.display."""
    try:
        from wcwidth import wcwidth
    except ImportError:                        # pyte's own dependency; never here
        return list(range(screen.columns))
    row = screen.buffer[y]
    cols, stub = [], False
    for x in range(screen.columns):
        if stub:
            stub = False
            continue
        data = row[x].data
        cols.extend([x] * len(data))
        stub = bool(data) and wcwidth(data[0]) == 2
    return cols

def screen_has_colour(screen):
    """Does anything on screen carry a colour? Claude with colour off (NO_COLOR,
    TERM=dumb, a CI environment) draws none at all, and then an error row looks
    exactly like prose: detect_state must not demand a colour that cannot be
    there. With colour on, its prompt box and hint row are coloured, so a scan
    from the bottom stops within a line or two."""
    buf = screen.buffer
    for y in range(screen.lines - 1, -1, -1):
        row = buf[y]
        for x in range(screen.columns):
            fg = row[x].fg
            if fg and fg != "default":
                return True
    return False

def colour_phrases(screen, display, phrases):
    """{display line: {phrase: coloured}} for every phrase that OPENS its row
    (after the indent and at most one glyph); `coloured` says whether it
    starts on a cell Claude coloured as an error or a warning. Call under
    screen_lock. Only lines that contain a phrase at all are looked at, so a
    frame without one costs a substring test per line. A pattern's own
    surrounding spaces do not move where it anchors."""
    out = {}
    phrases = [(p, p.strip()) for p in phrases if p and p.strip()]
    if not phrases:
        return out
    buf = screen.buffer
    for y, line in enumerate(display):
        # Lower-case one character at a time so indices still line up with
        # the cells ("İ".lower() is two characters).
        low = "".join(c.lower() if len(c.lower()) == 1 else c for c in line)
        found = [(p, core) for p, core in phrases if core in low]
        if not found:
            continue
        cols = _display_columns(screen, y)
        for p, core in found:
            i = low.find(core)
            if i >= 0 and _ROW_START_RE.match(line[:i]):
                x = cols[i] if i < len(cols) else i
                out.setdefault(line, {})[p] = _wf_role(buf[y][x].fg) in _ALERT_ROLES
    return out

class WorkflowWatch:
    """What one frame cannot say about workflow rows. Monitor thread only.

    1. Whether a row has ENDED while its text still looks live. An error-
       coloured row is either still going with one failed agent (its clock
       ticks) or has ended badly and is only lingering (its clock froze); a row
       whose colour cannot be read at all (a terminal that drops it) is judged
       the same way. The clock's resolution drops as a run ages -- "2m39s"
       ticks every second, "1h02m" every minute, "1d03h" every hour -- so
       "still" means still for longer than one tick.
    2. Whether rows that VANISHED took their workflows with them. A run always
       shows its outcome before its row goes (green, or frozen red, for ~30 s),
       so rows that disappear while still live were usually HIDDEN: a dialog
       replaced the prompt box (the workflow's own detail view among them), or
       help, shell mode, a suggestion list or exit-confirm took the panel's
       place. While Claude's normal prompt footer is away (prompt_footer_up)
       the reading holds for as long as that lasts. With the footer back and
       still no row, the run really is gone -- evicted before its colour could
       be read, or paused from /workflows -- and the reading lets go after
       GONE_S, a margin for a paste or a redraw.
    3. Whether the workflows it has watched were SEEN to end (`settled`), which
       is what retires a "Waiting for 1 dynamic workflow to finish" banner that
       nothing will ever supersede: a run you stop prints no completion line.
       Rows merely gone from view for a while settle nothing.
    4. What each run's clock reads NOW (`elapsed`), for the device: the text
       on screen is a floor that only moves once a tick, and while the rows are
       held out of view there is no text at all. Hence it keeps when each row's
       clock text last changed, and when the rows were last on screen.
    5. Which rows the daemon is told about (`rows`, for wf_field): kept longer
       than the verdict above, and finished ones too. The daemon shows a
       finished run for a minute from the first line that lists it ended, and
       keeps it hidden after that for as long as lines still list it -- but a
       record missing from one line and back in the next is a NEW run there:
       its minute starts over, and the device's band blinks.
       Finished rows go out of view just as live ones do, and for one reason
       more: a frame caught mid-redraw, the footer drawn and the rows not yet.
       So whatever their status the rows are kept while the footer is away,
       and for WF_REDRAW_S after it is back without them -- with all the watch
       knows of each, so a red row it had read as stopped is not taken for a
       running one again after a blink. None of this keeps the session
       working: only `active` does that, as above.
    6. When each run was SEEN to end (`ended`), for a panel with more rows
       than a report carries (wf_field): the frame that first read finished a
       row it had read running. A run that ends beside three running ones is
       never shown as ended unless it goes out as ended. A row first read
       already finished gets no stamp: it ended before anyone looked, and has
       no claim to have just ended.

    Rows are told apart by name AND position among rows of that name, not by
    name alone: a run launched again while its failed predecessor still
    lingers shows the same name twice, and two clocks filed under one key
    took turns resetting each other, so neither could ever read as stopped.
    """
    GONE_S = 20.0

    def __init__(self):
        self._clock = {}          # (name, n-th such name) -> (clock text, when it changed)
        self._ended = {}          # ...-> when it was seen to end (None: it never was)
        self._live = False        # were the rows live the last time any showed?
        self._last = 0.0          # ...and when they last showed, or the footer was away
        self._seen = False        # has any workflow row ever been on screen?
        self._outcome = False     # did the rows, last seen, show every run ENDED?
        self.active = False       # the last observe() verdict
        self.rows = []            # [(row, status)] from the last frame with rows, kept (5)
        self.ticked = []          # ...when each one's clock text last changed
        self.ended = []           # ...when each one was seen to end, if it was (6)
        self.read_at = 0.0        # ...and when that frame was
        self.lost = False         # did the last frame's reading fail? (detect_state)

    @property
    def settled(self):
        """Every run the rows showed was SEEN to end -- not merely gone from
        the screen for a while, which is what a menu or a dialog does too."""
        return self._seen and self._outcome and not self.active

    @staticmethod
    def _still_after(clock):
        if clock.endswith("h"):
            return 3600.0 + WF_FROZEN_S       # "1d03h": ticks every hour
        if clock.endswith("m"):
            return 60.0 + WF_FROZEN_S         # "1h02m": ticks every minute
        return WF_FROZEN_S                    # "2m39s" / "45s": every second

    @staticmethod
    def _tick(clock):
        """How long the clock's text stands still while the run goes on."""
        if clock.endswith("h"):
            return 3600.0
        if clock.endswith("m"):
            return 60.0
        return 1.0

    @staticmethod
    def _keys_of(rows):
        """[(row, anything)] -> each row's key: (name, rows of that name above it)."""
        keys, count = [], {}
        for r, _x in rows:
            keys.append((r["name"], count.get(r["name"], 0)))
            count[r["name"]] = keys[-1][1] + 1
        return keys

    def _status(self, row, role, now, key):
        st = wf_status(row, role)
        clock = row["clock"]
        if clock is not None:
            seen = self._clock.get(key)
            if seen is None or seen[0] != clock:
                self._clock[key] = (clock, now)
            elif now - seen[1] >= self._still_after(clock):
                if st == "E":
                    st = "F"                  # red, clock stopped: failed/stopped
                elif st == "R" and not role:  # no colour to go on, clock stopped
                    st = "D" if row["total"] and row["done"] == row["total"] else "F"
        return st

    def observe(self, rows, box, now):
        """rows: [(row, role)] read from this frame's chrome; box: whether
        Claude's normal prompt footer is on screen (prompt_footer_up), the only
        frame in which missing rows can count down. True while a workflow should
        keep the session working."""
        self.lost = False
        self.active = self._observe(rows, box, now)
        return self.active

    def _observe(self, rows, box, now):
        if not box:
            self._last = now                  # footer away: every countdown waits
            return self._live
        if rows:
            self._seen = True
            keys = self._keys_of(rows)
            was_live = {k for k, (_r, st) in zip(self._keys_of(self.rows), self.rows)
                        if st in _WF_LIVE}
            stats = [(r, self._status(r, role, now, key))
                     for (r, role), key in zip(rows, keys)]
            kept = set(keys)
            for key in [k for k in self._clock if k not in kept]:
                del self._clock[key]
            # An end is stamped once, by the frame that first reads the row
            # finished -- and only if the rows read before it showed it
            # running (6).
            self._ended = {k: (self._ended[k] if k in self._ended
                               else now if k in was_live else None)
                           for (_r, st), k in zip(stats, keys) if st not in _WF_LIVE}
            self.rows = stats
            self.ticked = [self._clock[k][1] if r["clock"] is not None else None
                           for (r, _st), k in zip(stats, keys)]
            self.ended = [self._ended.get(k) for k in keys]
            self.read_at = now
            self._live = any(st in _WF_LIVE for _r, st in stats)
            self._outcome = not self._live
            self._last = now
            return self._live
        if self._live and now - self._last < self.GONE_S:
            return True
        self._live = False
        if self.rows and now - self._last < WF_REDRAW_S:
            return False                      # likely mid-redraw: keep them (5)
        self.rows = []
        self.ticked = []
        self.ended = []
        self._clock.clear()
        self._ended.clear()
        return False

    def elapsed(self, i, now):
        """Row i's run clock at `now`, in whole seconds; None if it shows none.

        "2m39s" means 159 s and some fraction, "1h02m" anything up to 3779 s.
        A running row's clock has moved on since its text last changed: by up
        to one tick while it is on screen -- a text later than that has simply
        not ticked yet, and one that stays late reads as ended (above) -- and by
        all the time since it was last seen while it is held out of view, where
        nobody can watch it tick. A row that is not running (P/D/F) shows a
        clock that has stopped, so it is taken as it reads."""
        row, st = self.rows[i]
        secs, clock = row.get("elapsed_s"), row.get("clock")
        changed = self.ticked[i] if i < len(self.ticked) else None
        if secs is None or clock is None or changed is None or st not in ("R", "E"):
            return secs
        return int(secs + min(self.read_at - changed, self._tick(clock))
                   + max(0.0, now - self.read_at))

def _wf_name(name):
    """A workflow name as the wire carries it. It is a record's LAST
    sub-field, so a ';' in it is harmless; a '|' would end the whole field
    and a '~' the record, and a control character -- or one that some reader
    would take for a line break -- has no business on a device."""
    out = []
    for ch in name if isinstance(name, str) else "":
        if ch == "|":
            ch = "/"
        elif ch == "~":
            ch = "-"
        elif unicodedata.category(ch) in ("Cc", "Cs", "Zl", "Zp"):
            continue
        out.append(ch)
    return "".join(out)[:WF_NAME_MAX]

def _wf_record(row, st, secs):
    """One record: st;done;total;failed;pct;elapsed_s;tokens;note;name."""
    def num(v):
        return "" if v is None else str(int(v))
    pct = row.get("pct")
    note = "".join(c for c in (row.get("note") or "")
                   if " " <= c < "\x7f" and c not in ";~|")[:WF_NOTE_MAX]
    return ";".join((st, num(row.get("done")), num(row.get("total")), "",
                     num(None if pct is None else min(100, pct)), num(secs),
                     num(row.get("tokens")), note, _wf_name(row.get("name"))))

def wf_field(watch, now):
    """Field 8 of a report: the workflows `watch` can see, or is holding.

    '-' when there are none -- and before any was ever seen; '' ("keep what
    you have", to the daemon) when this frame's reading failed. Otherwise up
    to WF_MAX_RECORDS records joined by '~', each
        st;done;total;failed;pct;elapsed_s;tokens;note;name
    st is R E P D or F (wf_status, WorkflowWatch) and elapsed_s the run's
    clock NOW (WorkflowWatch.elapsed). A number the row does not show is ''
    -- `failed` always is: no row shows it.

    With more rows than that, which go: first a run seen to end in the last
    WF_JUST_ENDED_S, the latest first -- for that long the daemon gives the
    band to a run that has just ended, and a run ending beside three running
    ones, left off, was never shown as ended at all; then the running ones,
    since a run that ended a minute ago must not push a live one off the
    wire; then the other finished ones, the most recently ended first -- the
    oldest are the next Claude drops anyway, and one dropped here only to be
    listed again later would be a new run to the daemon, its minute started
    over. The
    picked ones go out in the panel's own (start-time) order. The field
    stays within WF_FIELD_MAX bytes by leaving out the last-picked records
    whole: a record cut short would name a different workflow."""
    if watch.lost:
        return ""
    if not watch.rows:                         # none -- or no longer kept
        return "-"
    rows = watch.rows
    ended = [watch.ended[i] if i < len(watch.ended) else None for i in range(len(rows))]
    over = sorted((i for i, (_r, st) in enumerate(rows) if st not in _WF_LIVE),
                  key=lambda i: float("inf") if ended[i] is None else -ended[i])
    fresh = [i for i in over if ended[i] is not None and now - ended[i] <= WF_JUST_ENDED_S]
    order = (fresh + [i for i, (_r, st) in enumerate(rows) if st in _WF_LIVE]
             + [i for i in over if i not in fresh])
    picked, size = [], -1                      # -1: n records, n - 1 '~'
    for i in order[:WF_MAX_RECORDS]:
        row, st = rows[i]
        rec = _wf_record(row, st, watch.elapsed(i, now))
        size += len(rec.encode("utf-8", errors="replace")) + 1
        if size > WF_FIELD_MAX:
            break
        picked.append((i, rec))
    if not picked:                             # not even one fits: no news
        return ""
    return "~".join(rec for _i, rec in sorted(picked))

def wf_shape(field):
    """What a report has to go out for at once: each record minus its clock
    and its tokens, and how many records there are. Those two move every
    second, and a report per tick would bury the daemon's log, so they ride
    the KEEPALIVE_S report instead (the daemon keeps the clock running in
    between)."""
    if field in ("", "-"):
        return field
    return tuple(tuple(p[:5] + p[7:])
                 for p in (rec.split(";", 8) for rec in field.split("~")))

def wf_report(watch, now):
    """(field 8, its shape) for one pass of evaluate(). Fenced: whatever goes
    wrong in here costs the workflow field -- ('', None): the daemon keeps
    what it has, and there is nothing new to report -- never the state
    report it rides on."""
    try:
        field = wf_field(watch, now)
        return field, (wf_shape(field) if field else None)
    except Exception:
        return "", None

def _banner_live(transcript_text, wf_watch):
    """_fresh(_BG_WORK_RE, ...) -- except for a banner that names ONLY dynamic
    workflows once WorkflowWatch has watched them all end.

    A finished workflow normally supersedes its banner itself: Claude prints the
    completion line and starts a turn on it. A run you STOP prints nothing, so
    "Waiting for 1 dynamic workflow to finish" stayed Claude's last word and held
    the session on WORK for good, its row long gone. The row is the better
    witness: once it has been seen to end, a banner that is only about
    workflows is over too. One that also names agents still counts -- an agent
    finishing does print a line."""
    last = None
    for last in _BG_WORK_RE.finditer(transcript_text):
        pass
    if last is None or _NEW_OUTPUT_RE.search(transcript_text[last.end():]):
        return False
    if wf_watch is not None and wf_watch.settled:
        said = last.group(0).lower()
        if "workflow" in said and not re.search(r"agent|tool|task", said):
            return False
    return True

def detect_state(screen_text, wf_fg=None, wf_watch=None, now=None,
                 err_cells=None, err_colour=True):
    # Read only Claude's LIVE STATUS REGION (the bottom of the visible screen),
    # not the whole scrollback. Error/prompt/generating chrome renders there, so
    # the same keywords appearing in conversation / logs / code / file content
    # higher up no longer false-trigger the state.
    #
    # Returns (state, source). `source` lets evaluate() debounce a footer-only
    # "waiting" (generic picker) longer than a specific permission prompt, so a
    # config dialog you open and dismiss can't buzz.
    #
    # `wf_fg` ({line: (bullet fg, name fg)}, from wf_bullet_colours),
    # `wf_watch` (a WorkflowWatch), `err_cells` ({line: {phrase: coloured}},
    # from colour_phrases) and `err_colour` (does the screen carry colour at
    # all) are what evaluate() knows beyond the text; without them the screen
    # is read from its text alone.
    nonblank = [ln for ln in screen_text.splitlines() if ln.strip()]
    transcript, chrome = split_screen(nonblank)

    # Dynamic-workflow rows (see parse_wf_row) are read here and then taken OUT
    # of every window below. They are names the user or the model chose, sitting
    # in exactly the bottom lines those windows scan -- a run named after "the
    # api error" would read ERROR -- and a stack of them pushes "? for
    # shortcuts" out of the footer window, and the idle gate with it.
    # All of it is fenced off: a row nobody anticipated must cost the workflow
    # reading, never the session's whole state report.
    wf_live = False
    try:
        wf = []
        if chrome is not None:
            base = len(nonblank) - len(chrome)
            drop = set()
            for j, ln in enumerate(chrome):
                row = parse_wf_row(ln)
                if row is None:
                    continue
                role = _wf_bullet_role(wf_fg.get(ln)) if wf_fg else ""
                if row["bare"] and (role != "warning"
                                    or re.search(r"\bmode on\b|shift\+tab", row["name"])):
                    continue                   # the mode indicator, not a run
                wf.append((row, role))
                drop.add(base + j)
            if drop:
                nonblank = [ln for i, ln in enumerate(nonblank) if i not in drop]
                chrome = [ln for j, ln in enumerate(chrome) if base + j not in drop]
        if wf_watch is not None:
            # A row on screen is proof enough that the footer is up -- Claude
            # draws the rows under its normal prompt footer and nowhere else
            # -- whatever prompt_footer_up makes of the rest of the frame. Held
            # instead of read, a row that turned green went on being reported
            # as a run still going.
            wf_live = wf_watch.observe(wf, bool(wf) or prompt_footer_up(nonblank),
                                       time.monotonic() if now is None else now)
        else:
            wf_live = any(wf_status(r, role) in _WF_LIVE for r, role in wf)
    except Exception:
        if wf_watch is not None:
            wf_watch.lost = True       # ...and the report says "unknown" ('')
                                       # rather than repeat an older frame's rows

    tail = "\n".join(nonblank[-STATUS_TAIL_LINES:]).lower()
    busy_tail = "\n".join(nonblank[-BUSY_TAIL_LINES:]).lower()
    footer = "\n".join(nonblank[-FOOTER_LINES:]).lower()
    full = "\n".join(nonblank)               # whole visible screen (for live lines)

    # Where the live activity METER may be read. It is Claude's own foreground
    # status line and always renders directly ABOVE the prompt box, so the region
    # is the bottom of the upper half plus the hint row (which carries "esc to
    # interrupt"). What it deliberately EXCLUDES is the AGENTS PANEL Claude lists
    # under the hint row: a finished subagent's row keeps its final tally on
    # display -- captured verbatim, 27s after the agent had returned --
    #   "◯ Explore  Count .h files in /usr/include        9s · ↓ 7.5k tokens"
    # and that dead meter read as live work for as long as the panel stayed up.
    meter_region = (busy_tail if transcript is None else
                    "\n".join((transcript + chrome[:1])[-BUSY_TAIL_LINES:]).lower())

    # Is the session actively working? Two signals:
    #  1. The live activity meter / "esc to interrupt" / background-work hints in
    #     the bottom BUSY_TAIL_LINES region (the normal foreground spinner spot).
    #  2. A glyph-prefixed LIVE status/agent line ANYWHERE on screen -- a running
    #     agent panel pushes the spinner above the bottom region (its transcript
    #     fills the screen below), which otherwise mis-reads as idle.
    # Compute this FIRST so it gates the content-prone error/picker checks below:
    # a working frame must never be mistaken for an error or a prompt.
    #
    # None of these count while Claude's IDLE-at-prompt footer is showing: a live
    # spinner/meter and a QUOTED one are textually identical, so the robust tell is
    # that the idle prompt ("? for shortcuts") is only present when the session is
    # waiting for input -- a real working frame (foreground OR a running agents
    # panel) never shows it. Gating the whole signal on `not idle_prompt` kills the
    # meter-quote false positives (a pasted status line, a README blockquote, this
    # repo's own fixtures) whether they land in the bottom region or higher up.
    idle_prompt = any(p in footer for p in _IDLE_FOOTER)
    live_work = (not idle_prompt) and (
                any(p in busy_tail for p in _patterns["busy"])
                or bool(_WORKING_METER_RE.search(meter_region))
                or bool(_LIVE_SPINNER_RE.search(full)))
    # Background work (a dynamic workflow, an agent, a backgrounded shell, a
    # monitor, an MCP task -- running OR still queued) keeps the session "working"
    # even though the foreground turn ended and the prompt is idle. Claude shows
    # it four ways, and none of them are gated on the idle prompt (which is up
    # WHILE the background work runs). Each is read against the half of the screen
    # it belongs to (see split_screen), which is what keeps it honest:
    #   1. the "Waiting for N ... to finish" banner (turn end, agents/workflows),
    #   2. the turn recap's "· N ... still running" suffix (turn end, the rest),
    #      and 3. the task panel's "N in background" counter -- all three are
    #      TRANSCRIPT text, so each counts only while _fresh(): Claude has printed
    #      nothing since that supersedes it. Without that gate they keep asserting
    #      work that finished long ago and the device never leaves WIP;
    #   4. the in-flight chip in the hint row -- live chrome below the box, the
    #      one tell that is also true for work that is merely QUEUED, and the one
    #      that survives the transcript lines scrolling away.
    # ...and a fifth, for the one kind of work the chip leaves out:
    #   5. a dynamic workflow's own row in the panel under the hint row, live
    #      while its bullet says it is (wf_live, above). Once the turn that
    #      launched it has ended it is often the ONLY tell left on screen.
    if transcript is None:
        # No prompt box on screen (a full-screen picker/dialog). We cannot tell
        # Claude's chrome from its output, so trust no transcript tell and read
        # the chip from the plain footer window. A workflow seen running before
        # the dialog came up is still running behind it (WorkflowWatch).
        bg_work = bool(_BG_CHIP_RE.search(footer)) or wf_live
    else:
        transcript_text = "\n".join(transcript)
        bg_work = (bool(_BG_CHIP_RE.search("\n".join(chrome)))
                   or _banner_live(transcript_text, wf_watch)
                   or _fresh(_BG_TURN_END_RE, transcript_text)
                   or _fresh(_BG_COUNT_RE, transcript_text)
                   or wf_live)
    busy_now = live_work or bg_work

    # Strong, specific error strings win even mid-stream (a genuine API error
    # replaces the spinner). Content-prone error phrases match ONLY in the tight
    # bottom region AND never while the meter shows work in progress -- otherwise
    # the meter's own digits (e.g. "↓ 500 tokens") or a number on screen would
    # read as a false HTTP error that then nags every 5s.
    #
    # With the cells to go on (colour_phrases), a phrase counts only where it
    # opens a row: in Claude's chrome below the prompt box that is enough;
    # above its lower rule (the conversation, and the line you are typing) it
    # must also be in an error colour -- unless the screen has no colour at
    # all (err_colour False) -- and the row must still be current: no
    # submitted prompt echoed after it. With no cells (text only), a plain
    # substring match as before.
    chrome_at = (len(nonblank) - len(chrome)) if chrome is not None else 0
    box_at = len(transcript) if transcript is not None else 0

    def current(i):
        return not any(_PROMPT_ECHO_RE.match(ln) for ln in nonblank[i + 1:box_at])

    def error_in(window, phrases):
        first = len(nonblank) - window
        for i, ln in enumerate(nonblank[-window:], start=max(0, first)):
            low = ln.lower()
            for p in phrases:
                if p not in low:
                    continue
                if err_cells is None:
                    return True
                hit = err_cells.get(ln, {})
                if p not in hit:
                    continue                   # mid-row: code, a diff, prose
                if i >= chrome_at or ((hit[p] or not err_colour) and current(i)):
                    return True
        return False

    if error_in(STATUS_TAIL_LINES, _patterns["error"]):
        return ("error", "error")
    if not busy_now and error_in(BUSY_TAIL_LINES, _patterns.get("error_footer", [])):
        return ("error", "error")
    if any(p in tail for p in _patterns["waiting"]):
        return ("waiting", "specific")
    # A genuine picker is NOT generating: the live "esc to interrupt" / meter hint
    # wins over generic picker words that may be incidental near the bottom.
    # Only the FOREGROUND spinner vetoes it, though -- background work says
    # nothing about whether a picker is up, and a session that keeps a shell or a
    # workflow running would otherwise stop reporting that it needs you.
    if not live_work and any(p in footer for p in _patterns.get("waiting_footer", [])):
        return ("waiting", "footer")
    if busy_now:
        return ("working", "busy")
    return ("idle", "idle")

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Locate the real `claude` (avoid recursing into this wrapper)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def real_claude():
    explicit = os.environ.get("CLAUDE_REAL")
    if explicit and os.path.exists(explicit):
        return explicit
    me = os.path.realpath(sys.argv[0])
    wrapper = os.path.realpath(__file__)
    for d in os.environ.get("PATH", "").split(os.pathsep):
        cand = os.path.join(d, "claude")
        if not (os.path.isfile(cand) and os.access(cand, os.X_OK)):
            continue
        rp = os.path.realpath(cand)
        if rp == me or rp == wrapper:
            continue
        # Skip our own shim (a tiny script that calls this wrapper) so a `claude`
        # shim earlier on PATH can't cause infinite double-wrapping.
        #
        # Match ANY name this wrapper has ever shipped under, not just the
        # current one. The check greps the candidate for the wrapper's own
        # filename, which quietly makes the detection depend on the wrapper
        # never being renamed: rename it, and a user whose old shim is still on
        # PATH gets an infinite re-exec with no output and nothing to go on.
        # Cheap to make rename-proof now, and a trap to leave in place.
        LEGACY_NAMES = (b"claude-mate-wrap",)
        try:
            if os.path.getsize(cand) < 8192:
                with open(cand, "rb") as fh:
                    blob = fh.read()
                if any(n in blob for n in LEGACY_NAMES):
                    continue
        except OSError:
            pass
        return cand
    # Last resort: the canonical install dir, so the wrapper still finds the real
    # binary even when EVERY `claude` on PATH (incl. ~/.local/bin/claude) is our
    # shim. Pick the newest version directory's executable.
    vdir = os.path.expanduser("~/.local/share/claude/versions")
    try:
        for v in sorted(os.listdir(vdir), reverse=True):
            cand = os.path.join(vdir, v)
            if os.path.isfile(cand) and os.access(cand, os.X_OK):
                return cand
    except OSError:
        pass
    return None  # not found -- caller must handle (NEVER fall back to a bare
                 # "claude": in a global-shim deploy that re-execs THIS wrapper
                 # -> infinite re-exec loop, esp. on the pyte-missing path).

def exec_real_claude(extra_argv):
    """exec the real claude (skipping this shim) with our argv, or exit cleanly if
    it can't be located. Never execs a bare "claude" -- that would re-enter the
    wrapper and, when pyte is missing, spin forever."""
    rc = real_claude()
    if not rc:
        sys.stderr.write("[claude-mate-wrap] could not locate the real `claude` "
                         "binary; set CLAUDE_REAL=/path/to/claude.\n")
        os._exit(127)
    os.execvp(rc, ["claude"] + extra_argv)
    os._exit(127)

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Account profiles — pick a CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR before exec'ing claude
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Claude Code keeps ONE login per config dir ($CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR, default
# ~/.claude + ~/.claude.json). Pointing different wrapped terminals at different
# config dirs is the only way to hold different accounts at once. Profiles live
# as subdirectories of ACCOUNTS_DIR; "default" means no override (~/.claude).
# Opt-in: no profile dirs -> no picker, the wrapper behaves exactly as before.
ACCOUNTS_DIR = os.path.expanduser(
    os.environ.get("CLAUDE_MATE_ACCOUNTS_DIR", "~/.claude-accounts"))

def account_email(cfg_dir=None):
    """Best-effort email of the account logged into a config dir ('' if none).
    The default profile's state file is ~/.claude.json (NOT inside ~/.claude);
    with CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR set, claude writes it as <dir>/.claude.json."""
    path = (os.path.join(cfg_dir, ".claude.json") if cfg_dir
            else os.path.expanduser("~/.claude.json"))
    try:
        with open(path) as fh:
            return (json.load(fh).get("oauthAccount") or {}).get("emailAddress", "")
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return ""

def email_domain(email):
    """The organisation half of a login, lowercased ('' if there is none).
    Two accounts whose domains differ are two tenancies, which is the one
    distinction a switch is not allowed to make quietly."""
    return email.rsplit("@", 1)[-1].lower() if "@" in (email or "") else ""

def list_profiles():
    try:
        return sorted(d for d in os.listdir(ACCOUNTS_DIR)
                      if os.path.isdir(os.path.join(ACCOUNTS_DIR, d)))
    except OSError:
        return []

def strip_account_flag(argv):
    """Pull our --account NAME / --account=NAME out of argv so it never reaches
    the real claude (which would reject the unknown flag)."""
    out, name, i = [], None, 0
    while i < len(argv):
        a = argv[i]
        if a == "--account" and i + 1 < len(argv):
            name, i = argv[i + 1], i + 2
            continue
        if a.startswith("--account="):
            name, i = a.split("=", 1)[1], i + 1
            continue
        out.append(a)
        i += 1
    return name, out

def use_profile(name):
    """Point the child claude at profile `name` (created on first use; claude
    shows /login there on first run). 'default' / '' keeps ~/.claude."""
    if not name or name == "default":
        return
    d = os.path.join(ACCOUNTS_DIR, name)
    try:
        os.makedirs(d, exist_ok=True)
    except OSError as e:
        sys.stderr.write(f"[claude-mate-wrap] cannot create profile {d}: {e}\n")
        os._exit(1)
    os.environ["CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR"] = d

# The shared chooser, if it is reachable. Imported softly because this file is
# also loaded as a module by claude-mate-switch and by the tests, and a missing
# sibling must degrade to the typed prompt rather than break the wrapper that
# every session runs through.
_PICK_CANCELLED = object()
try:
    _uidir = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
    if _uidir not in sys.path:
        sys.path.insert(0, _uidir)
    import claude_mate_ui as _ui
except Exception:                                    # noqa: BLE001
    _ui = None


def _pick_account(profiles):
    """The account picker, with arrow keys. A profile name, "" for default,
    None if nothing was chosen, or _PICK_CANCELLED on esc/ctrl-C."""
    while True:
        items = [_ui.Item("default", value="",
                          detail=account_email() or "(not logged in)")]
        for p in profiles:
            email = account_email(os.path.join(ACCOUNTS_DIR, p))
            # show_name's rule, locally: a name that is not plainly printable
            # is shown as a repr so the row is at least identifiable.
            label = p if all(32 <= ord(c) < 127 for c in p) else repr(p)
            items.append(_ui.Item(label, value=p,
                                  detail=email or "(not logged in)"))
        items.append(_ui.Item("New account…", value=_NEW, gap=True,
                              detail="create a profile and log in there"))
        # `d` IS HERE BECAUSE THIS PROMPT IS THE ONLY CLAUDE MATE UI ON THE MAC.
        # A device that will not link says so on its own glass and in the
        # daemon's log, neither of which is in front of the person here.
        items.append(_ui.Item("Device…", value=_DEVICE,
                              detail="connect a Claude Mate, or see why it is not"))
        got = _ui.pick("claude-mate — which account?", items,
                       hint="↑↓ move   ⏎ start   esc cancel")
        if got is None:
            return _PICK_CANCELLED
        if got is _DEVICE:
            rc = _run_connect()
            if rc == 0:
                print("claude-mate: device is linked.\n")
            continue
        if got is _NEW:
            name = _ask_new_profile()
            if name:
                return name
            continue
        return got


def _ask_new_profile():
    """Type a name, deliberately, having chosen to create one.

    Typing is right HERE and wrong at the picker: this is the one place where a
    new string is the actual answer, and you got here by choosing to. The name
    is still validated, because "\\033" must not become a directory however you
    arrived at it.
    """
    try:
        name = input("\n  new account name: ").strip()
    except (EOFError, KeyboardInterrupt):
        print()
        return None
    if not name:
        return None
    if not _plausible_profile(name):
        print(f"  ignoring {name!r} — that does not look like an account name.")
        try:
            input("  press enter to go back ")
        except (EOFError, KeyboardInterrupt):
            pass
        return None
    return name


_NEW = object()
_DEVICE = object()


def select_account(flag_name, interactive):
    """Decide which account profile this session runs under.
    Precedence: existing $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR (never second-guess an explicit
    env) > --account flag > $CLAUDE_MATE_ACCOUNT > interactive picker (only
    when profiles exist) > default. Runs BEFORE the PTY fork / raw mode, so
    plain print/input are safe here and nothing is registered with the daemon
    yet if the user aborts with Ctrl-C."""
    if os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR"):
        return
    name = flag_name or os.environ.get("CLAUDE_MATE_ACCOUNT")
    if name is not None:
        use_profile(name)
        return
    profiles = list_profiles()
    if not interactive or not profiles:
        return
    # ARROWS FIRST, TYPING AS THE FALLBACK. This prompt is the one Claude Mate
    # screen everybody sees, on every session, and it was the last one still
    # asking you to read a number off a list and type it.
    #
    # It is also where the damage came from. Typing was how you chose, so an
    # arrow key -- the thing a person naturally reaches for at a list -- typed
    # an ESCAPE SEQUENCE into the name field and CREATED A PROFILE CALLED
    # "\033", which then rendered as a blank row with somebody's email beside
    # it and could not be typed back to delete. The maintainer's machine had
    # one, and a "в" from a keyboard left in the wrong layout. A chooser cannot
    # do that to you.
    #
    # The typed path below is kept verbatim for everything that is not a
    # terminal on both ends -- a test harness feeding stdin, a script, a
    # terminal that cannot do escape sequences -- because losing it would mean
    # this function only worked in the one case it is easiest to test in.
    if _ui and _ui.supports_ui():
        chosen = _pick_account(profiles)
        if chosen is _PICK_CANCELLED:
            print()
            os._exit(130)
        if chosen:
            use_profile(chosen)
        return
    # `d` IS HERE BECAUSE THIS PROMPT IS THE ONLY CLAUDE MATE UI ON THE MAC.
    # A device that will not link says so on its own glass and in the daemon's
    # log, and neither is anywhere the person at this prompt is looking -- so
    # "how do I connect the device" had no answer reachable from the one screen
    # they always see. It does not select anything: it prints the state of every
    # link and the shortest fix, then comes back here.
    while True:
        print("claude-mate — select account (Enter = default, or type a new name):")
        print(f"  0) default   {account_email() or '(not logged in)'}")
        for i, p in enumerate(profiles, 1):
            email = account_email(os.path.join(ACCOUNTS_DIR, p))
            print(f"  {i}) {p:<10} {email or '(not logged in)'}")
        print("  d) device     connect a Claude Mate, or see why it is not linked")
        try:
            ans = input("account> ").strip()
        except (EOFError, KeyboardInterrupt):
            print()
            os._exit(130)
        if ans.lower() in ("d", "device"):
            rc = _run_connect()
            # Say where we ended up, ON THE LINE ABOVE THE PROMPT. The device
            # itself now shows a PAIRED receipt, and the terminal that asked for
            # it should not be the quieter of the two.
            if rc == 0:
                print("claude-mate: device is linked.\n")
            continue                     # ...and ask again; nothing was chosen
        break
    if not ans or ans == "0":
        return
    if ans.isdigit() and 1 <= int(ans) <= len(profiles):
        use_profile(profiles[int(ans) - 1])
        return
    if ans in profiles:
        use_profile(ans)
        return
    # A NEW NAME CREATES A PROFILE, SO IT HAS TO BE ASKED FOR. Anything typed
    # here used to become one, and an arrow key types an ESCAPE SEQUENCE -- so a
    # real accounts dir ended up with a directory named `\033`, which prints as
    # a blank row with somebody's email beside it, and another named `в` from a
    # keyboard left in the wrong layout. Both were logged into afterwards,
    # because the picker had already committed and claude asked for a login.
    if not _plausible_profile(ans):
        print(f"  ignoring {ans!r} — that does not look like an account name.")
        return
    try:
        yn = input(f'  "{ans}" is new. Create a profile for it? [y/N] ').strip()
    except (EOFError, KeyboardInterrupt):
        print()
        os._exit(130)
    if yn.lower() not in ("y", "yes"):
        print("  staying on the default account.")
        return
    use_profile(ans)


def _plausible_profile(name):
    """Could this be a profile name someone meant to type?

    Deliberately narrow: printable, no path separators (a name becomes a
    DIRECTORY under ~/.claude-accounts, so `../` there is not a naming choice),
    and short enough to be a name rather than a paste.
    """
    return (name
            and len(name) <= 32
            and all(32 <= ord(c) < 127 for c in name)
            and "/" not in name and "\\" not in name
            and name not in (".", ".."))


def _run_connect():
    """Hand over to claude-mate-connect, which owns everything about linking.

    A subprocess rather than an import: the tool is a hyphenated script with no
    module name, it is useful on its own, and keeping the knowledge of transports
    out of the wrapper is the whole reason it is a separate file. Beside this one
    first, so a repo checkout runs its own copy rather than an older installed
    one."""
    here = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)),
                        "claude-mate-connect")
    cmd = [sys.executable, here] if os.path.exists(here) else ["claude-mate-connect"]
    try:
        rc = subprocess.run(cmd, check=False).returncode
    except OSError as e:
        sys.stderr.write(f"[claude-mate-wrap] cannot run claude-mate-connect: {e}\n")
        rc = 1
    print()
    return rc

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Account + remaining-limit reporting (shown on the device's r1/r2 right edges)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# The wrapper knows which account this session runs as (selected above) and can
# read that account's usage. Claude Code stores the OAuth access token per
# config dir: in <cfg>/.credentials.json where that file exists, else in the
# macOS Keychain under service "Claude Code-credentials" -- with a
# "-<sha256(cfg_dir)[:8]>" suffix when CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR is set (verified
# against a real profile). The token is only ever READ; refreshing stays
# claude's job (refresh tokens rotate, so writing here could log the account
# out from under it).
USAGE_URL = "https://api.anthropic.com/api/oauth/usage"
try:
    USAGE_POLL_S = float(os.environ.get("CLAUDE_MATE_USAGE_POLL_S", "120"))
except ValueError:
    USAGE_POLL_S = 120.0

def account_label():
    """Short account name for the device: the CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR basename (the
    profile name for ~/.claude-accounts/<name>), or 'default' for ~/.claude."""
    cfg = os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR", "")
    if not cfg:
        return "default"
    return os.path.basename(os.path.normpath(cfg)).lstrip(".") or "default"

def _access_token(cfg=None):
    """OAuth access token for a config dir, or ''. File first (Linux / explicit
    installs), then the Keychain (the macOS default).

    `cfg` defaults to THIS session's config dir. It is a parameter so
    claude-mate-switch can read another profile's remaining limit without
    running as that profile -- picking a switch target is exactly the question
    "which of my accounts still has headroom", and answering it needs every
    profile's token, not just the one we happen to be inside."""
    if cfg is None:
        cfg = os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR", "")
    path = os.path.join(cfg or os.path.expanduser("~/.claude"),
                        ".credentials.json")
    raw = ""
    try:
        with open(path) as fh:
            raw = fh.read()
    except OSError:
        service = "Claude Code-credentials"
        if cfg:
            service += "-" + hashlib.sha256(cfg.encode()).hexdigest()[:8]
        try:
            out = subprocess.run(
                ["security", "find-generic-password", "-s", service, "-w"],
                capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=5)
            raw = out.stdout.strip() if out.returncode == 0 else ""
        except Exception:
            return ""
    try:
        return ((json.loads(raw) or {}).get("claudeAiOauth")
                or {}).get("accessToken", "") or ""
    except ValueError:
        return ""

def _fetch_usage(token):
    """GET the usage endpoint via curl, not urllib: curl uses the system trust
    store (python.org builds often ship without CA certs and would fail TLS
    verification), and the token travels via a stdin config file, never argv
    (argv is world-readable in `ps`). Returns the parsed JSON or None."""
    cfg = (f'url = "{USAGE_URL}"\n'
           f'header = "Authorization: Bearer {token}"\n'
           'header = "anthropic-beta: oauth-2025-04-20"\n')
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(["curl", "-sS", "-m", "10", "-K", "-"],
                             input=cfg, capture_output=True, text=True,
                             timeout=15)
        if out.returncode != 0:
            return None
        return json.loads(out.stdout)
    except Exception:
        return None

def _format_limit(data):
    """Compact remaining-limit chip for the OLED, <= 6 chars. The BINDING
    window (whichever has less left) wins: '5h82%' = 82% of the 5-hour window
    remaining, 'wk31%' = 31% of the week left. '' if usage is unavailable
    (console/API-key accounts have no plan windows)."""
    try:
        r5 = 100.0 - float(data["five_hour"]["utilization"])
        r7 = 100.0 - float(data["seven_day"]["utilization"])
    except (KeyError, TypeError, ValueError):
        return ""
    r5 = max(0, min(100, round(r5)))
    r7 = max(0, min(100, round(r7)))
    return f"wk{r7}%" if r7 < r5 else f"5h{r5}%"

def usage_poller():
    """Refresh g_meta['limit'] every USAGE_POLL_S. On any failure (offline,
    expired token, no plan limits) the last-known value is KEPT: claude
    refreshes the token as it runs, so the next poll heals on its own. The
    monitor thread notices the changed g_meta and reports it to the daemon."""
    while True:
        tok = _access_token()
        if tok:
            lim = _format_limit(_fetch_usage(tok) or {})
            if lim:
                g_meta["limit"] = lim
        time.sleep(max(30.0, USAGE_POLL_S))

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Session context tracker — what claude-mate-switch needs to move you
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# An account IS a $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR, chosen before exec'ing claude and fixed
# for the life of the process. So "switch account" can only ever mean "start
# claude again somewhere else", and the thing that makes that seamless rather
# than a restart is knowing WHICH conversation was in flight in THIS terminal.
#
# Hence a tracker, and hence one file per terminal rather than one per user: two
# wrapped terminals in the same directory are two different conversations, and a
# switch in one must not drag the other's transcript along. The tty name is the
# only identifier that means "this terminal" to both the wrapper and a command
# the user types into it afterwards.
#
# TMP ON PURPOSE. This is live state about a running process -- it is worthless
# once the process is gone and actively misleading if it outlives it. Kept under
# the temp dir so a reboot clears it, written 0600 in a 0700 directory (it names
# working directories and transcript paths), and unlinked by cleanup() on the
# way out. Stale files from a crash are ignored by mtime, not trusted.
CTX_DIR = os.path.join(tempfile.gettempdir(), f"claude-mate-ctx-{os.getuid()}")
CTX_STALE_S = 900.0          # a context not refreshed for this long is dead

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Which conversation is THIS one — asked, not guessed
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Everything above hangs off one fact: which transcript file belongs to this
# terminal. Claude Code names it <session-id>.jsonl, so the honest way to know
# is to CHOOSE the id and hand it to claude (`--session-id`), instead of looking
# at a directory afterwards and picking the newest file. That guess was wrong in
# the field, and wrong in the worst possible way: two terminals, two projects,
# and a switch that copied the OTHER project's 7 MB conversation into a
# different organisation's account.
#
# Only a fresh session can be told its id. --resume <id> already names one;
# --continue, a bare --resume and --from-pr do not, and claude rejects
# --session-id next to them -- those fall back to the (now verified) scan below.
#
# Whether a given claude HAS that flag is remembered, never waited for. The only
# way to ask is to run `claude --help`, and nothing may sit in front of a
# terminal you just opened: a binary that answers slowly -- or is not really
# claude and answers never -- would hold the session there. So a cold cache
# means "no id this time" (the verified scan below still works) and the answer
# is fetched in the background for the next session. It changes only when the
# binary does.
def caps_path():
    return os.path.join(CTX_DIR, "claude-caps.json")

def _caps_key(binary):
    try:
        st = os.stat(binary)
    except OSError:
        return ""
    return f"{binary}:{int(st.st_mtime)}:{st.st_size}"

def _caps_load():
    """{binary-key: has-the-flag}. One entry per binary rather than one entry
    full stop: a machine can have more than one claude on it (an upgrade
    mid-flight, a $CLAUDE_REAL pointing somewhere else), and a single-slot cache
    would have them evicting each other forever."""
    try:
        with open(caps_path()) as fh:
            blob = json.load(fh)
        return blob if isinstance(blob, dict) else {}
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return {}

def claude_caps_cached(binary):
    """True / False for a remembered answer, None if we have never asked."""
    key = _caps_key(binary)
    if not key:
        return None
    val = _caps_load().get(key)
    return None if val is None else bool(val)

def probe_session_id_support(binary):
    """Ask the binary and remember the answer. Returns what it found.

    stdin is /dev/null on purpose: this runs while a session is live, and a
    subprocess sharing the terminal's stdin would eat the user's keystrokes."""
    key = _caps_key(binary)
    if not key:
        return False
    try:
        out = subprocess.run([binary, "--help"], capture_output=True,
                             stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=20)
    except Exception:
        return False                      # probe never ran: remember nothing
    ok = b"--session-id" in (out.stdout or b"") + (out.stderr or b"")
    blob = _caps_load()
    blob.pop(key, None)
    blob = dict(list(blob.items())[-3:])        # the last few binaries, no more
    blob[key] = ok
    try:
        os.makedirs(CTX_DIR, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
        tmp = caps_path() + f".{os.getpid()}.tmp"
        with open(tmp, "w") as fh:
            json.dump(blob, fh)
        os.replace(tmp, caps_path())            # atomic: two sessions may probe
    except OSError:
        pass
    return ok

def warm_claude_caps():
    """Fetch the answer for next time, off the startup path. Called from the
    parent AFTER the PTY fork -- a thread running across fork() is how a child
    ends up deadlocked on a lock nobody will release."""
    rc = real_claude()
    if rc and claude_caps_cached(rc) is None:
        threading.Thread(target=probe_session_id_support, args=(rc,),
                         daemon=True).start()

def session_argv(argv):
    """(argv to give claude, the session id we will KNOW it is using).

    Returns the id unchanged when the user's own flags already name one, ''
    when claude alone decides it (--continue, bare --resume, --fork-session),
    and otherwise mints one and passes it down."""
    named, resuming, fork = "", False, False
    for i, a in enumerate(argv):
        nxt = argv[i + 1] if i + 1 < len(argv) else ""
        if a == "--session-id" and nxt:
            named = named or nxt
        elif a.startswith("--session-id="):
            named = named or a.split("=", 1)[1]
        elif a in ("--resume", "-r"):
            resuming = True
            if nxt and not nxt.startswith("-"):
                named = named or nxt          # --resume <id>: that IS the id
        elif a.startswith("--resume="):
            resuming = True
            named = named or a.split("=", 1)[1]
        elif a in ("--continue", "-c") or a == "--from-pr" or a.startswith("--from-pr="):
            resuming = True
        elif a == "--fork-session":
            fork = True                       # claude mints an id we are not told
    if fork:
        return argv, ""
    if named:
        return argv, named
    if resuming:
        return argv, ""
    rc = real_claude()
    if not rc or claude_caps_cached(rc) is not True:
        return argv, ""       # unknown or too old: the scan below still works
    sid = str(uuid.uuid4())
    return argv + ["--session-id", sid], sid

def ctx_key(tty=None):
    """Identify a terminal. The tty device name with the path stripped; a
    pid-derived fallback for the (wrapped-but-not-a-tty) cases that never reach
    the tracker anyway, so the key can never be empty."""
    t = MY_TTY if tty is None else tty
    return os.path.basename(t) if t else f"pid-{os.getpid()}"

def ctx_path(tty=None):
    return os.path.join(CTX_DIR, ctx_key(tty) + ".json")

def project_dir_names(cwd):
    """The directory names Claude Code might have mangled this cwd into.

    The rule is undocumented and it is NOT just '/' -> '-': '_' and '.' are
    mangled too, so /Users/x/Projects/claude_mate lives in
    -Users-x-Projects-claude-mate. Getting that wrong is what broke the old
    guess -- the name never matched, so the search fell through to "newest
    .jsonl anywhere". Hence a SET of candidates, used only to order the search
    and to break a tie for a transcript too young to say where it belongs;
    never as the answer on its own."""
    return {cwd.replace("/", "-"), re.sub(r"[/._]", "-", cwd)}

def transcript_cwd(path, lines=12):
    """The working directory Claude Code recorded INSIDE a transcript, or ''.

    The only authority on which directory a conversation belongs to, and the
    reason this no longer has to trust a filename. The first records are session
    metadata with no cwd, so read a few lines rather than one."""
    try:
        with open(path, errors="ignore") as fh:
            for _ in range(lines):
                line = fh.readline()
                if not line:
                    break
                try:
                    rec = json.loads(line)
                except ValueError:
                    continue
                if isinstance(rec, dict) and rec.get("cwd"):
                    return rec["cwd"]
    except OSError:
        pass
    return ""

def find_transcript(cfg_dir, cwd, since=0.0, sid=""):
    """This session's transcript file, or ''.

    Claude Code writes <cfg>/projects/<mangled-cwd>/<session-id>.jsonl. With an
    id in hand (the normal case now -- see session_argv) the file is simply the
    one with that name, wherever the mangling put it: no heuristic, no tie to
    break, and correct even for a session that has not typed anything yet.

    Without one, fall back to scanning -- but VERIFY. A candidate is accepted
    only if the cwd recorded inside it is this one; a file too new to have
    recorded anything is accepted only from a directory whose name is a
    plausible mangling of this cwd. The old version took the newest .jsonl from
    any project directory, which is how a switch in one terminal walked off with
    another terminal's conversation."""
    root = os.path.join(cfg_dir or os.path.expanduser("~/.claude"), "projects")
    try:
        entries = os.listdir(root)
    except OSError:
        return ""

    if sid:
        for name in entries:
            p = os.path.join(root, name, sid + ".jsonl")
            if os.path.exists(p):
                return p
        return ""

    names = project_dir_names(cwd)
    here = os.path.realpath(cwd)
    best, best_mtime = "", since
    order = ([e for e in entries if e in names]
             + [e for e in entries if e not in names])
    for name in order:
        d = os.path.join(root, name)
        try:
            listing = os.listdir(d)
        except OSError:
            continue
        for fn in listing:
            if not fn.endswith(".jsonl"):
                continue
            p = os.path.join(d, fn)
            try:
                m = os.path.getmtime(p)
            except OSError:
                continue
            if m <= best_mtime:
                continue
            rec = transcript_cwd(p)
            if rec:
                if os.path.realpath(rec) != here:
                    continue          # another directory's conversation
            elif name not in names:
                continue              # unverifiable AND not our project dir
            best, best_mtime = p, m
    return best

def ctx_write(state="", started=0.0):
    """Publish this terminal's context. Best-effort and never fatal: the tracker
    is a convenience for a command the user may never run, and must not be able
    to take down a working session."""
    try:
        os.makedirs(CTX_DIR, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
        cfg = os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR", "")
        transcript = find_transcript(cfg, os.getcwd(), started, g_claude_sid)
        blob = json.dumps({
            "tty": MY_TTY,
            "pid": os.getpid(),
            "cwd": os.getcwd(),
            "account": account_label(),
            "config_dir": cfg,
            "transcript": transcript,
            "session_id": (os.path.basename(transcript)[:-6] if transcript else ""),
            # The flags this session was started with, so a switch can resume it
            # as the same kind of session rather than a default one.
            "argv": list(g_user_argv),
            "state": state,
            "limit": g_meta.get("limit", ""),
            "model": g_meta.get("model", ""),
            "started_at": started,
            "updated_at": time.time(),
        }, indent=2)
        # Atomic: a switch running concurrently must never read half a file.
        p = ctx_path()
        tmp = p + f".{os.getpid()}.tmp"
        with open(tmp, "w") as fh:
            fh.write(blob)
        os.chmod(tmp, 0o600)
        os.replace(tmp, p)
    except Exception:
        pass

def ctx_clear():
    try:
        os.unlink(ctx_path())
    except OSError:
        pass

def switch_argv(req):
    """The switcher's argv for a `switch` request.

    An account name is ONE argument, however many spaces are in it: a profile is
    a directory the user named, and "work 2" is a perfectly ordinary name for
    one. Splitting on whitespace turned it into two arguments and the switcher
    rejected the second -- after claude had already been killed for it. Only a
    flag request ('--best') is a list of words."""
    req = (req or "").strip()
    return req.split() if req.startswith("-") else [req]

def switch_refusal(req):
    """Why a switch request cannot be honoured, or '' if it can.

    Answered BEFORE claude is asked to exit. The wrapper has to end the session
    to switch it, so a refusal discovered on the way out costs you the session
    and gives nothing back: pressing the device's account button in a session
    that had not spoken yet killed claude, printed "no transcript recorded for
    this terminal yet", and left a shell prompt. Every reason that can be known
    up front is checked up front."""
    name = "" if req.startswith("-") else req      # otherwise it is a flag
    if name:
        if name == account_label():
            return f"already on {name}"
        if name != "default" and not os.path.isdir(os.path.join(ACCOUNTS_DIR, name)):
            return f"no such account: {name}"
        # NO CROSS-ORGANISATION REFUSAL HERE, and the reason is worth keeping.
        #
        # This used to compare the two logins' email domains and turn the switch
        # away when they differed, on the theory that moving a conversation
        # between tenancies is a decision a button cannot make. The theory was
        # wrong about WHICH accounts are reachable. Every target is a login the
        # user installed and authenticated themselves -- `default` is ~/.claude,
        # and the rest are directories under ACCOUNTS_DIR that exist only
        # because someone ran a login in them. There is no untrusted tenancy on
        # the menu; there is only "your accounts, at whichever companies you
        # work for".
        #
        # So the check could not distinguish the case it was written for from
        # the case the feature exists for, and in practice only ever saw the
        # latter: three profiles on three domains, every pair "cross-org", every
        # device switch refused -- silently, because the refusal only reached a
        # log file. A guard that fires on 100% of legitimate use is not a guard,
        # it is an outage. The domain change is now ANNOUNCED by the switcher
        # (see claude-mate-switch) rather than used to refuse.
        #
        # What still refuses is everything above and below: an account you are
        # already on, one that does not exist, and a session with nothing to
        # carry -- all answered before claude is killed for the attempt.
    if not find_transcript(os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR", ""),
                           os.getcwd(), g_started_at, g_claude_sid):
        # Short on purpose: these words now go on the device's screen, which is
        # 21 characters wide and gets two rows for a message. A sentence with
        # advice in it wrapped to "nothing to carry yet / -- say something to c+"
        # and the advice was the half that got cut.
        return "nothing to carry yet"
    return ""

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Main
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
def main():
    # Account selection first: it must apply on EVERY exec path below (wrapped,
    # pyte-missing, non-interactive), and must finish before raw mode / PTY fork.
    flag_name, argv = strip_account_flag(sys.argv[1:])
    sys.argv[1:] = argv
    select_account(flag_name,
                   interactive=sys.stdin.isatty() and sys.stdout.isatty())
    g_meta["account"] = account_label()   # ride along on every daemon report
    # pyte is required to mirror the TUI; without it we cannot wrap. Run the real
    # claude unwrapped via real_claude() (which skips THIS shim) -- never bare
    # "claude", which in the global-shim deployment would re-exec the wrapper
    # forever (the import-time fallback's old bug).
    if pyte is None:
        sys.stderr.write("[claude-mate-wrap] pyte not installed (pip install pyte); "
                         "running claude unwrapped.\n")
        exec_real_claude(sys.argv[1:])

    # Non-interactive (piped / scripted) use: don't wrap, just be claude. This
    # keeps a global `claude` shim safe for `claude -p ...`, CI, pipes, etc.
    if not (sys.stdin.isatty() and sys.stdout.isatty()):
        exec_real_claude(sys.argv[1:])

    # Name the conversation before starting it, so the tracker never has to
    # guess which transcript is ours (see session_argv). Deliberately AFTER the
    # two unwrapped exec paths above: an unwrapped claude feeds no tracker and
    # must run exactly as the user typed it. g_user_argv keeps the user's own
    # flags so a switch can resume as the same kind of session.
    global g_user_argv, g_claude_sid
    g_user_argv = list(sys.argv[1:])
    sys.argv[1:], g_claude_sid = session_argv(sys.argv[1:])

    rows, cols = get_winsize(sys.stdin.fileno())

    pid, master_fd = pty.fork()
    if pid > 0:
        global g_child_pid
        g_child_pid = pid          # so a `switch` can ask claude to stand down
        warm_claude_caps()         # for the NEXT session; costs this one nothing
    if pid == 0:
        # Child: become the real claude.
        exec_real_claude(sys.argv[1:])

    # Parent.
    set_winsize(master_fd, rows, cols)
    # Non-blocking writes to the child's PTY (#3). A large paste while claude is
    # busy can fill the PTY input buffer; a BLOCKING os.write(master_fd, ...) would
    # then stall the relay -> the wrapper stops draining claude's output -> claude
    # blocks on ITS write -> two-way deadlock. With the fd non-blocking we buffer
    # the remainder (`pend`) and flush it when select() reports master_fd writable.
    # Reads become non-blocking too, so master-side reads guard for BlockingIOError.
    os.set_blocking(master_fd, False)

    # Set our unique terminal title (for focus matching) — harmless if ignored.
    try:
        sys.stdout.write(f"\033]0;{TITLE}\007")
        sys.stdout.flush()
    except Exception:
        pass

    screen = pyte.Screen(cols, rows)
    stream = pyte.ByteStream(screen)
    screen_lock = threading.Lock()

    # meta_reported: model, effort, account, limit and the workflow field's
    # SHAPE (wf_shape) as last reported -- '-', as the launch report says.
    state = {"cur": "idle", "reported": None, "pending": None,
             "pending_src": "idle", "since": 0.0, "last_sent": 0.0,
             "meta_reported": ("", "", "", "", "-"),
             "colour": True, "colour_at": -1e9}
    dbg = open(DEBUG, "a") if DEBUG else None
    wf_watch = WorkflowWatch()                 # touched by evaluate() only

    def evaluate():
        global g_screen_text
        now = time.monotonic()
        with screen_lock:                      # all of it needs the live cells
            display = screen.display
            try:
                wf_fg = wf_bullet_colours(screen, display)
                err_cells = colour_phrases(
                    screen, display,
                    _patterns["error"] + _patterns.get("error_footer", []))
                if now - state["colour_at"] >= 10.0:   # a session's colour depth
                    state["colour"] = screen_has_colour(screen)   # does not change
                    state["colour_at"] = now
            except Exception:                  # colours are a refinement: fall
                wf_fg = err_cells = None       # back to reading the text alone
        text = "\n".join(display)
        g_screen_text = text                   # publish for the mirror view
        parse_meta(text)                       # refresh cached model/effort
        new, src = detect_state(text, wf_fg, wf_watch, now, err_cells,
                                state["colour"])
        # The workflows, as of this frame, for whichever report goes out next.
        # Fenced (wf_report): a failure here costs the field -- '' = keep --
        # and is no reason to report; the state report goes out regardless.
        g_meta["wf"], shape = wf_report(wf_watch, now)
        if shape is None:
            shape = state["meta_reported"][4]
        if new != state["pending"]:
            state["pending"] = new
            state["since"] = now
        state["pending_src"] = src             # latest source governs the hold
        # A footer-only "waiting" must persist longer than a normal transition
        # so a config dialog you open and dismiss (/effort, /model) never reports.
        hold = (WAITING_FOOTER_DEBOUNCE_S
                if (state["pending"] == "waiting" and src == "footer")
                else DEBOUNCE_S)
        # Promote a pending state once it has been stable long enough.
        if state["pending"] != state["cur"] and (now - state["since"]) >= hold:
            state["cur"] = state["pending"]
        # Report on a state change, when model/effort/account/limit first
        # become known (or change), when the workflows change SHAPE -- one
        # starts, ends, pauses, gains a done agent -- or as a periodic
        # keepalive. A workflow's clock and token count change every second
        # and are not a reason to report: they ride the keepalive.
        meta = (g_meta["model"], g_meta["effort"],
                g_meta["account"], g_meta["limit"], shape)
        if (state["cur"] != state["reported"] or meta != state["meta_reported"]
                or (now - state["last_sent"]) >= KEEPALIVE_S):
            state["reported"] = state["cur"]
            state["meta_reported"] = meta
            state["last_sent"] = now
            send_daemon(state["cur"])
            if dbg:
                dbg.write(f"\n===== {time.strftime('%H:%M:%S')} state={state['cur']} "
                          f"model={meta[0]!r} effort={meta[1]!r} "
                          f"wf={g_meta['wf']!r} =====\n")
                dbg.write(text + "\n")
                dbg.flush()

    # SIGWINCH handling -- keep PTY + emulator sized to the real terminal.
    #
    # The terminal sends SIGWINCH on every resize (and many emulators raise it
    # when you open/split a tab as the layout reflows). Python delivers signals
    # on the MAIN thread, between bytecodes -- so a handler that takes
    # `screen_lock` can SELF-DEADLOCK: if the resize lands while the main loop
    # is inside `with screen_lock: stream.feed(...)` (very likely during heavy
    # output) the handler runs on that same thread and blocks forever trying to
    # re-acquire a non-reentrant lock the thread already holds. That froze the
    # whole session on resize / new-tab, forcing a kill (and losing output).
    #
    # Fix: the handler does only what is async-signal-safe -- flip a flag (a
    # single store) -- and `set_wakeup_fd` pokes a self-pipe so a blocked
    # select() returns promptly. The ACTUAL resize (ioctl + pyte) runs back in
    # the main loop, OUTSIDE signal context, where taking the lock is safe.
    winch_pending = [False]
    wake_r, wake_w = os.pipe()
    os.set_blocking(wake_r, False)
    os.set_blocking(wake_w, False)
    global g_wake_w
    g_wake_w = wake_w              # let queue_input() wake this loop immediately

    def on_winch(signum, frame):
        winch_pending[0] = True       # async-signal-safe: just flag it
    signal.signal(signal.SIGWINCH, on_winch)
    try:
        # Make any signal wake select() (main thread only); drop the byte if the
        # pipe ever backs up rather than spamming stderr.
        signal.set_wakeup_fd(wake_w, warn_on_full_buffer=False)
    except (ValueError, OSError, TypeError):
        pass

    def apply_resize():
        r, c = get_winsize(sys.stdin.fileno())
        set_winsize(master_fd, r, c)          # resize PTY -> child claude reflows
        with screen_lock:
            try:
                screen.resize(r, c)           # keep the emulator in step
            except Exception:
                pass

    threading.Thread(target=ctrl_listener, daemon=True).start()
    if USAGE_POLL_S > 0:
        threading.Thread(target=usage_poller, daemon=True).start()
    send_daemon("idle")  # register immediately on launch

    # Periodic evaluator (catches quiescence after output stops). `_running` lets
    # cleanup() stop it BEFORE it sends the final 'end', so the monitor can't fire
    # a keepalive/state report afterwards and re-register the just-ended session
    # (a ghost the daemon would then keep alive via last_update_ts).
    _running = [True]
    # The transcript does not exist yet at launch -- claude creates it on the
    # first turn -- so the tracker is refreshed from the monitor rather than
    # written once. `_started` is what stops find_transcript() from adopting a
    # stale file that some earlier session left in this directory.
    _started = time.time()
    global g_started_at
    g_started_at = _started            # the ctrl thread needs it too, to answer
    ctx_write("idle", _started)        # "is there anything here to carry yet?"
    def monitor():
        while _running[0]:
            time.sleep(POLL_MS)
            if not _running[0]:
                break
            try:
                load_patterns()   # hot-reload tuning without a restart
                evaluate()
                ctx_write(state["cur"], _started)
            except Exception:
                pass
    threading.Thread(target=monitor, daemon=True).start()

    old = None
    try:
        old = termios.tcgetattr(sys.stdin)
        tty.setraw(sys.stdin.fileno())
    except Exception:
        old = None

    # --- Single cleanup path, run on normal exit AND on external termination ---
    # Previously ALL teardown lived in the `finally` below, which `kill`, logout
    # (SIGHUP) or a `kill -QUIT` skip by default -> the shell came back with no
    # echo / no line editing (blind `reset` needed), the daemon kept showing the
    # dead session for up to its TTL, and the ctrl socket leaked. Now one
    # idempotent cleanup() restores the tty, tells the daemon, and unlinks the
    # socket -- registered via atexit and the TERM/HUP/QUIT handlers below.
    _cleaned = [False]
    def cleanup():
        if _cleaned[0]:
            return
        _cleaned[0] = True
        _running[0] = False                             # stop the monitor (no post-end report)
        if not g_switch_req:
            ctx_clear()                                 # live state, worthless once we exit
        try:
            signal.set_wakeup_fd(-1)
        except Exception:
            pass
        # CRITICAL teardown FIRST -- deregister and drop the socket before any
        # terminal-touching step, so a wedged / flow-controlled (Ctrl-S) terminal
        # can never leave a ghost session behind: the 'end' must always get out.
        send_daemon("end")                              # deregister this session
        try:
            if os.path.exists(CTRL_SOCK):
                os.unlink(CTRL_SOCK)
        except OSError:
            pass
        # Hang up the child's PTY. If the relay broke while claude was still alive
        # (e.g. our output terminal vanished), closing the master sends the child
        # EOF/SIGHUP so it exits and the waitpid below can reap it instead of
        # blocking forever.
        try:
            os.close(master_fd)
        except OSError:
            pass
        for _fd in (wake_r, wake_w):
            try:
                os.close(_fd)
            except OSError:
                pass
        if dbg:
            try:
                dbg.close()
            except Exception:
                pass
        # Restore the tty with TCSANOW (apply immediately). TCSADRAIN/TCSAFLUSH
        # would BLOCK until the terminal's output queue drains -- exactly the
        # wedged-terminal case this teardown must survive -- so they are wrong here.
        if old is not None:
            try:
                termios.tcsetattr(sys.stdin, termios.TCSANOW, old)
            except Exception:
                pass
        # Cosmetic title reset LAST and NON-BLOCKING: if the terminal's output
        # buffer is full (no reader / Ctrl-S), a blocking write here would hang the
        # entire exit. Best-effort only -- it must never stall teardown.
        try:
            ofd = sys.stdout.fileno()
            fl = fcntl.fcntl(ofd, fcntl.F_GETFL)
            fcntl.fcntl(ofd, fcntl.F_SETFL, fl | os.O_NONBLOCK)
            try:
                os.write(ofd, b"\033]0;\007")           # clear our title (#13)
            except OSError:
                pass
            fcntl.fcntl(ofd, fcntl.F_SETFL, fl)
        except Exception:
            pass

    def _on_term(signum, frame):
        # External termination: run the same teardown as a clean exit, then
        # re-raise the signal's default disposition so our exit status is correct
        # and the child gets reaped. cleanup() never takes screen_lock, so (unlike
        # the old SIGWINCH bug) running it from a signal handler can't deadlock.
        cleanup()
        try:
            signal.signal(signum, signal.SIG_DFL)
            os.kill(os.getpid(), signum)
        except Exception:
            os._exit(128 + signum)

    for _sig in (signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGHUP, signal.SIGQUIT):
        try:
            signal.signal(_sig, _on_term)
        except (ValueError, OSError):
            pass
    atexit.register(cleanup)

    # Bytes queued for the child's PTY (master_fd), drained NON-BLOCKING so a big
    # paste can never stall the relay (#3).
    pend = bytearray()
    def flush_to_child():
        """Push as much of `pend` to the child as the PTY will accept right now.
        Returns False on a fatal write error (caller should exit)."""
        while pend:
            try:
                n = os.write(master_fd, pend)
            except BlockingIOError:
                return True          # PTY input full; keep remainder, retry on writable
            except OSError:
                return False         # child / PTY gone
            if n <= 0:
                return True
            del pend[:n]
        return True

    out_fd = sys.stdout.fileno()
    def write_all_stdout(buf):
        """Write the WHOLE buffer to stdout, looping on the returned count. stdout
        stays blocking, but a write interrupted by a signal (e.g. SIGWINCH) after
        transferring some bytes returns a SHORT count -- CPython only auto-retries
        an EINTR that moved zero bytes -- so a single os.write could silently drop
        the tail (corrupting the visible TUI while pyte still sees the full buffer).
        Returns False on a fatal terminal error (caller should exit)."""
        mv = memoryview(buf)
        while mv:
            try:
                n = os.write(out_fd, mv)
            except InterruptedError:
                continue
            except OSError:
                return False
            if n <= 0:
                return False
            mv = mv[n:]
        return True

    stdin_fd = sys.stdin.fileno()
    stdin_open = True
    try:
        while True:
            # Apply any resize requested by the SIGWINCH handler, on THIS thread
            # (never inside the signal handler -> no self-deadlock on screen_lock).
            if winch_pending[0]:
                winch_pending[0] = False
                try:
                    apply_resize()
                except Exception:
                    pass

            # Text the device asked us to type, queued by the ctrl thread. Moved
            # into `pend` here so injected bytes go through exactly the same
            # non-blocking drain as your own keystrokes -- one writer, one
            # ordering, and a full PTY buffer cannot stall the relay.
            drain_inject(pend)

            rset = [master_fd, wake_r]
            if stdin_open:
                rset.append(stdin_fd)
            wset = [master_fd] if pend else []   # only wait for writable with a backlog
            try:
                # The self-pipe (set_wakeup_fd) normally wakes us instantly on a
                # SIGWINCH. The 1s timeout is a belt-and-suspenders fallback: if
                # set_wakeup_fd is ever unavailable, a pending resize is still
                # applied (top of loop) within ~1s instead of never -- without
                # busy-waiting when idle.
                r, w, _ = select.select(rset, wset, [], 1.0)
            except (OSError, select.error):
                break

            if wake_r in r:
                # Drain the signal-wakeup pipe; the winch flag (checked at the
                # top of the loop) carries the actual intent.
                try:
                    while os.read(wake_r, 4096):
                        pass
                except OSError:
                    pass

            if stdin_open and stdin_fd in r:
                try:
                    data = os.read(stdin_fd, 4096)
                except OSError:
                    data = b""
                if not data:
                    # stdin EOF (#7): stop selecting on it. The OLD loop only
                    # forwarded `if data:` and never dropped stdin, so after EOF
                    # select reported it readable forever -> 100% CPU busy-spin
                    # (e.g. under nohup/disown where SIGHUP doesn't kill). We keep
                    # relaying the child's output until the child itself exits.
                    stdin_open = False
                else:
                    pend += data
                    if not flush_to_child():
                        break

            if pend and master_fd in w:
                if not flush_to_child():
                    break

            if master_fd in r:
                try:
                    out = os.read(master_fd, 8192)
                except BlockingIOError:
                    out = None                 # spurious readable; nothing to do
                except OSError:
                    break
                if out == b"":
                    break                      # child closed the PTY -> exit
                if out:
                    if not write_all_stdout(out):      # full, partial-safe passthrough (#5)
                        break                  # terminal gone: exit so we reap the child
                    with screen_lock:
                        try:
                            stream.feed(out)   # mirror into the emulator
                        except Exception:
                            pass
            # State evaluation is the monitor thread's job ONLY (sole evaluator, #9):
            # no inline evaluate() here, so the two threads can't race the `state`
            # dict / debounce or tear a g_meta read. The monitor re-evaluates every
            # POLL_MS, which is plenty responsive for an away-from-keyboard alert.
    finally:
        cleanup()

    # A switch asked for from the device. Done HERE, at the very end, on purpose:
    # cleanup() has already restored the terminal, deregistered the session and
    # dropped the socket, so the switcher starts from a clean shell exactly as if
    # you had typed it -- and it is not replacing the process from inside a
    # socket handler mid-teardown. It reads the context file cleanup() was told
    # to leave behind, copies the transcript, and re-execs this wrapper with
    # --resume. os.execv never returns; if it fails we fall through and exit
    # normally rather than stranding the terminal.
    if g_switch_req:
        switcher = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)),
                                "claude-mate-switch")
        try:
            os.waitpid(pid, 0)                          # let claude finish exiting
        except OSError:
            pass
        try:
            sys.stdout.write("\r\n[claude-mate] switching account...\r\n")
            sys.stdout.flush()
        except Exception:
            pass
        # No --yes, and nothing for it to skip any more: the switcher announces
        # a change of organisation and proceeds, rather than stopping for a
        # typed confirmation. That matters HERE more than anywhere. This runs
        # after cleanup(), with claude already gone -- a prompt at this point
        # would strand the terminal at a question, having ended the session to
        # ask it, which is the same "no effect" the refusal used to produce.
        # Everything that can refuse has already refused, up front, while the
        # session was still alive.
        try:
            os.execv(sys.executable, [sys.executable, switcher]
                                     + switch_argv(g_switch_req))
        except OSError as exc:
            sys.stderr.write(f"[claude-mate-wrap] switch failed: {exc}\n")

    # Propagate claude's exit code. cleanup() has already closed master_fd, so a
    # child still alive on an abnormal break path gets SIGHUP and exits; we poll
    # briefly, then escalate to SIGKILL so the wrapper can NEVER block here forever
    # (the old unconditional os.waitpid(pid, 0) hung if the child ignored the
    # hangup). SystemExit from sys.exit() is not an Exception, so it propagates.
    try:
        for _ in range(50):                             # ~5s grace
            wpid, status = os.waitpid(pid, os.WNOHANG)
            if wpid:
                sys.exit(os.waitstatus_to_exitcode(status))
            time.sleep(0.1)
        try:
            os.kill(pid, signal.SIGKILL)
        except OSError:
            pass
        _, status = os.waitpid(pid, 0)
        sys.exit(os.waitstatus_to_exitcode(status))
    except Exception:
        sys.exit(0)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
